window.MEDIC_BLOG_POSTS=[{"title":"ABGs Made Simple: Understanding pH, CO₂, Oxygen, and Bicarbonate","slug":"abg-made-simple-acid-base-respiratory-metabolic","date":"2026-10-29","category":"Airway & Respiratory","description":"Learn how to read arterial blood gases using pH, PaCO₂, PaO₂, and HCO₃⁻, and recognize respiratory versus metabolic acidosis and alkalosis.","tags":["ABG","arterial blood gas","pH","PaCO2","PaO2","bicarbonate","respiratory acidosis","respiratory alkalosis","metabolic acidosis","metabolic alkalosis","acid base"],"html":"<p><strong>ABGs do not have to feel complicated.</strong> Start with one idea: <strong>the lungs regulate carbon dioxide (CO₂), the kidneys regulate bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻), and the pH tells us the overall acid–base direction.</strong> Oxygen (PaO₂) answers a different but equally important question: how well is oxygen getting into arterial blood?</p>\n<h2>The four numbers you need to know</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Typical adult reference range</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>pH</strong></td><td>7.35–7.45</td><td>Acidemia below 7.35; alkalemia above 7.45</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PaCO₂</strong></td><td>35–45 mmHg</td><td>Ventilation and the respiratory component of acid–base balance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>HCO₃⁻</strong></td><td>22–26 mEq/L</td><td>The metabolic component of acid–base balance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PaO₂</strong></td><td>About 80–100 mmHg at sea level on room air in a healthy young adult</td><td>Arterial oxygenation; interpretation depends on age, altitude, and inspired oxygen</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Reference ranges vary slightly by laboratory. Also note that an ABG measures pH, PaCO₂, and PaO₂ directly; the bicarbonate value is usually <strong>calculated</strong> from pH and PaCO₂.</p>\n<h2>Why does CO₂ affect acidity?</h2>\n<p>Think of the reversible reaction:</p><p><strong>CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻</strong></p>\n<p>When ventilation falls, CO₂ accumulates and tends to push the reaction toward more hydrogen ions (H⁺), <strong>lowering pH</strong>. When ventilation increases, more CO₂ is exhaled and pH tends to <strong>rise</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>CO₂ acts like an acid in this system:</strong> higher PaCO₂ tends to mean lower pH; lower PaCO₂ tends to mean higher pH. Bicarbonate is the major extracellular buffer: higher HCO₃⁻ tends to raise pH, while lower HCO₃⁻ tends to lower it.</p>\n<h2>Step 1: Is the blood acidic or alkaline?</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pH below 7.35:</strong> acidemia.</li><li><strong>pH above 7.45:</strong> alkalemia.</li><li><strong>pH 7.35–7.45:</strong> within the usual range. This does <em>not</em> always mean there is no acid–base disorder; compensation or mixed disorders can produce a near-normal pH.</li></ul>\n<p>Strictly speaking, <em>acidosis</em> and <em>alkalosis</em> describe processes; <em>acidemia</em> and <em>alkalemia</em> describe the measured blood pH.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Is CO₂ or bicarbonate driving the pH?</h2>\n<p>Ask which value moves in a direction that explains the pH:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Primary disorder</th><th>pH tendency</th><th>Primary change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Respiratory acidosis</strong></td><td>↓</td><td>PaCO₂ ↑</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Respiratory alkalosis</strong></td><td>↑</td><td>PaCO₂ ↓</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolic acidosis</strong></td><td>↓</td><td>HCO₃⁻ ↓</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Metabolic alkalosis</strong></td><td>↑</td><td>HCO₃⁻ ↑</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Memory aid:</strong> CO₂ and pH usually move in <em>opposite</em> directions in a primary respiratory disorder. Bicarbonate and pH usually move in the <em>same</em> direction in a primary metabolic disorder.</p>\n<h2>Respiratory acidosis: too much CO₂</h2>\n<p><strong>Example ABG:</strong> pH <strong>7.25</strong>, PaCO₂ <strong>60</strong> mmHg, HCO₃⁻ <strong>26</strong> mEq/L.</p>\n<p>The pH is low, so the patient is acidemic. PaCO₂ is high, explaining the low pH: <strong>primary respiratory acidosis</strong>. This can occur with hypoventilation due to opioid toxicity, severe obstructive lung disease, respiratory muscle weakness, or fatigue. The clinical question is not simply “Does the patient need oxygen?” but also <strong>“Is the patient ventilating adequately?”</strong></p>\n<h2>Respiratory alkalosis: too little CO₂</h2>\n<p><strong>Example ABG:</strong> pH <strong>7.52</strong>, PaCO₂ <strong>28</strong> mmHg, HCO₃⁻ <strong>23</strong> mEq/L.</p>\n<p>The pH is high and PaCO₂ is low: <strong>primary respiratory alkalosis</strong>. Causes can include hyperventilation from pain, anxiety, hypoxemia, pulmonary embolism, or early sepsis. <strong>Do not assume tachypnea is anxiety.</strong> It may be a response to serious underlying disease.</p>\n<h2>Metabolic acidosis: bicarbonate is low</h2>\n<p><strong>Example ABG:</strong> pH <strong>7.25</strong>, PaCO₂ <strong>25</strong> mmHg, HCO₃⁻ <strong>11</strong> mEq/L.</p>\n<p>The pH is low and bicarbonate is low: <strong>primary metabolic acidosis</strong>. Common causes include lactic acidosis from impaired perfusion, diabetic ketoacidosis, renal failure, and bicarbonate loss from diarrhea. The patient may breathe rapidly and deeply to blow off CO₂. Those respirations can be <strong>compensatory</strong>, not evidence that the lungs caused the problem.</p>\n<p>For metabolic acidosis, an expected PaCO₂ can be estimated using <strong>Winter's formula: (1.5 × HCO₃⁻) + 8 ± 2</strong>. If the measured PaCO₂ is substantially outside that range, consider a second respiratory disorder. The example above predicts roughly 22.5–26.5 mmHg; a PaCO₂ of 25 is consistent with expected compensation.</p>\n<h2>Metabolic alkalosis: bicarbonate is high</h2>\n<p><strong>Example ABG:</strong> pH <strong>7.50</strong>, PaCO₂ <strong>48</strong> mmHg, HCO₃⁻ <strong>36</strong> mEq/L.</p>\n<p>The pH is high and bicarbonate is high: <strong>primary metabolic alkalosis</strong>. Causes include prolonged vomiting or gastric suction, diuretic use, and certain states of volume or chloride depletion. The body may retain some CO₂ through reduced ventilation as compensation, but this response is limited by the need to maintain oxygenation.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: Understand compensation</h2>\n<p>The body tries to bring pH toward normal: the <strong>lungs can change CO₂ within minutes</strong>, while the <strong>kidneys adjust bicarbonate and acid excretion over hours to days</strong>. Compensation does not mean the underlying problem is fixed, and it usually does not push pH past normal on its own. A normal pH with abnormal PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ may represent a compensated or mixed disorder.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> A patient can have <em>more than one disorder at once</em>. For example, sepsis may cause metabolic acidosis from lactate accumulation along with respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. A simple four-category approach identifies common patterns, but does not replace checking compensation, anion gap, and clinical context.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: Do not confuse oxygenation with ventilation</h2>\n<p><strong>PaO₂ tells you about oxygenation; PaCO₂ tells you about ventilation.</strong> They are not interchangeable. A patient can have a satisfactory oxygen saturation while retaining dangerous amounts of CO₂, particularly when receiving supplemental oxygen. Pulse oximetry does not measure CO₂ or pH. End-tidal CO₂ (ETCO₂) helps assess ventilation and perfusion trends, but its value may differ from arterial PaCO₂, especially in shock or lung disease.</p>\n<h2>Practice: interpret these four patterns</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>pH 7.28 | PaCO₂ 58 | HCO₃⁻ 26:</strong> respiratory acidosis.</li><li><strong>pH 7.51 | PaCO₂ 29 | HCO₃⁻ 23:</strong> respiratory alkalosis.</li><li><strong>pH 7.22 | PaCO₂ 24 | HCO₃⁻ 10:</strong> metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation.</li><li><strong>pH 7.49 | PaCO₂ 47 | HCO₃⁻ 35:</strong> metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation.</li></ul>\n<h2>The field takeaway</h2>\n<p>Read the <strong>pH first</strong>. Then look at <strong>PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻</strong> to identify what best explains it. Check whether compensation makes sense, and evaluate <strong>PaO₂ separately</strong> for oxygenation. Most importantly, treat the <strong>patient and the underlying cause</strong>, not just an abnormal number.</p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK536919/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NCBI Bookshelf: Arterial Blood Gas</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.merckmanuals.com/professional/endocrine-and-metabolic-disorders/acid-base-regulation-and-disorders/acid-base-disorders\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Merck Manual Professional: Acid–Base Disorders</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for healthcare professionals and students. Interpret blood gases in clinical context and follow local protocols.</em></p>"},{"title":"The 28-Day Menstrual Cycle: Understanding the Hormones, Phases, and Physiology","slug":"28-day-menstrual-cycle-phases-hormones-ovulation","date":"2026-10-29","category":"Anatomy & Physiology","description":"A practical breakdown of menstrual, follicular, ovulatory, and luteal phases; FSH, LH, estrogen, and progesterone; and what clinicians should know about cycle variability.","tags":["menstrual cycle","ovulation","estrogen","progesterone","FSH","LH","follicular phase","luteal phase","reproductive physiology"],"html":"<p><strong>The menstrual cycle is more than a monthly period. It is a coordinated sequence of hormonal changes involving the brain, ovaries, and uterus.</strong> The familiar 28-day cycle is a teaching model, not a schedule that every person follows. Adult cycles commonly range from about 24 to 38 days, and timing can vary from month to month.</p>\n<h2>First, understand Day 1</h2>\n<p><strong>Day 1 is the first day of menstrual bleeding</strong> (not the last day of the previous period). The cycle ends the day before the next period begins. Menstruation is only one part of the full cycle.</p>\n<h2>Who controls the cycle?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> releases gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), signaling the <strong>anterior pituitary</strong> to release follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH). These act on the ovaries, which produce estrogen and progesterone. The ovarian hormones, in turn, influence the uterine lining and provide feedback to the brain.</p>\n<h2>Days 1–5: menstruation</h2>\n<p>In a textbook 28-day cycle, bleeding often begins around Days 1–5, although duration varies. If pregnancy has not occurred, the corpus luteum from the prior cycle breaks down. <strong>Progesterone and estrogen fall</strong>, causing the uterine lining (endometrium) to shed. This is the menstrual period.</p>\n<p>FSH begins supporting development of ovarian follicles for the new cycle. The uterus and ovaries have overlapping cycles: menstruation happens during the early <strong>follicular phase</strong> of the ovarian cycle.</p>\n<h2>Days 1–13: follicular phase</h2>\n<p>During the follicular phase, FSH stimulates follicles in the ovary. Usually one becomes dominant. Its developing cells produce increasing amounts of <strong>estradiol (estrogen)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Estrogen helps rebuild and thicken the uterine lining. This is called the <strong>proliferative phase</strong> of the uterine cycle. As estrogen rises, cervical mucus may become clearer, stretchier, and more favorable for sperm movement.</p>\n<p>The follicular phase is the part of the cycle that varies most in length, which is one reason ovulation does not reliably occur on the same calendar day in every cycle.</p>\n<h2>Around Day 14: ovulation in the 28-day model</h2>\n<p>When estradiol remains elevated long enough, its feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary shifts, triggering a surge of <strong>LH</strong>. This LH surge causes the mature follicle to release an egg: <strong>ovulation</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a textbook 28-day cycle, ovulation is often shown near Day 14. In real life, it can occur earlier or later. The egg remains viable for roughly 12–24 hours after ovulation; sperm may survive in the reproductive tract for up to about five days. This creates a fertile window that begins <em>before</em> ovulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Calendar dates alone are not reliable contraception</strong>, because the timing of ovulation varies.</p>\n<h2>Days 15–28: luteal phase</h2>\n<p>After ovulation, the ruptured follicle transforms into the <strong>corpus luteum</strong>, which secretes substantial amounts of <strong>progesterone</strong> along with estrogen.</p>\n<p>Progesterone transforms the thickened endometrium into a <strong>secretory lining</strong> that can support implantation. It also tends to increase basal body temperature slightly after ovulation.</p>\n<p>The luteal phase is generally less variable than the follicular phase, commonly lasting about 12–14 days, though normal variation exists.</p>\n<h2>What happens if pregnancy does—or does not—occur?</h2>\n<p><strong>If pregnancy does not occur:</strong> the corpus luteum regresses, progesterone and estrogen levels drop, and the uterine lining begins shedding. The next period marks Day 1 of a new cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>If implantation occurs:</strong> developing placental tissue produces <strong>human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)</strong>, which helps maintain the corpus luteum and progesterone production in early pregnancy. The endometrium is maintained rather than shed in a normal menstrual period.</p>\n<h2>The four key hormones at a glance</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Hormone</th><th>Main role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FSH</td><td>Stimulates ovarian follicle development</td></tr><tr><td>LH</td><td>LH surge triggers ovulation and supports corpus luteum formation</td></tr><tr><td>Estrogen (estradiol)</td><td>Rebuilds the uterine lining and helps trigger the LH surge</td></tr><tr><td>Progesterone</td><td>Supports the secretory uterine lining after ovulation</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Why this matters for EMS and healthcare professionals</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Do not assume every cycle is 28 days.</strong> Stress, illness, age, hormonal contraception, pregnancy, and other conditions can affect bleeding patterns and timing.</li><li><strong>Ask about the last menstrual period (LMP) thoughtfully.</strong> It may help assess pregnancy possibility, but an LMP alone cannot confirm or exclude pregnancy.</li><li><strong>Consider pregnancy-related emergencies.</strong> In a patient with reproductive potential, abdominal or pelvic pain, syncope, or vaginal bleeding may warrant concern for ectopic pregnancy, even when the patient reports recent bleeding.</li><li><strong>Assess the patient, not just the calendar.</strong> Severe bleeding, hemodynamic instability, or significant pain requires clinical evaluation regardless of the reported cycle day.</li></ul>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p><strong>FSH grows the follicle. Estrogen builds the lining. The LH surge triggers ovulation. Progesterone maintains the lining.</strong> When pregnancy does not occur, hormone levels fall and menstruation begins again. The 28-day diagram helps explain the sequence, but real cycles vary.</p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/your-menstrual-cycle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ACOG: Your Menstrual Cycle</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/menstruation/conditioninfo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NICHD: Menstruation and the menstrual cycle</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for healthcare professionals and students. Follow local clinical protocols and medical direction.</em></p>"},{"title":"Ischemic vs. Hemorrhagic Stroke: Same Symptoms, Different Emergencies","slug":"ischemic-vs-hemorrhagic-stroke-ems","date":"2026-10-29","category":"Clinical Education","description":"Understand ischemic versus hemorrhagic stroke, why symptoms overlap, and how EMS assessment, last-known-well time, and stroke-center decisions affect outcomes.","tags":["stroke","ischemic stroke","hemorrhagic stroke","EMS","BE FAST","LVO","intracerebral hemorrhage","subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"html":"<p><strong>A sudden facial droop, arm weakness, or speech disturbance tells you to suspect stroke. It does not tell you whether the brain has a blocked artery or a bleeding vessel.</strong> That difference changes hospital treatment, but the prehospital priorities are often the same: recognize the emergency, identify the timeline, assess for mimics, and get the patient to the right facility quickly.</p>\n<h2>Ischemic stroke: blood flow is blocked</h2>\n<p>An <strong>ischemic stroke</strong> occurs when an artery supplying the brain is obstructed, usually by a thrombus forming locally or an embolus traveling from elsewhere, such as the heart. The downstream brain tissue loses oxygen and glucose. Without adequate perfusion, cellular energy failure and injury follow.</p>\n<p>The central area of severe ischemia may become irreversibly injured, while surrounding <strong>penumbral tissue</strong> may still be salvageable if blood flow is restored in time. Ischemic strokes account for the large majority of strokes.</p>\n<h2>Hemorrhagic stroke: a vessel ruptures</h2>\n<p>A <strong>hemorrhagic stroke</strong> involves bleeding in or around the brain. Two important types are:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH):</strong> bleeding directly into brain tissue, which can damage tissue, increase pressure, and impair surrounding perfusion.</li><li><strong>Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH):</strong> bleeding into the space surrounding the brain, often associated with a ruptured aneurysm. A sudden, severe thunderclap headache is a classic warning sign, although presentations vary.</li></ul>\n<p>Uncontrolled hypertension, vascular abnormalities, and anticoagulant use can be relevant risk factors, but risk factors alone do not establish the stroke type.</p>\n<h2>Why can they look the same?</h2>\n<p>Both a blocked artery and bleeding can disrupt the function of the affected brain region. Either can cause unilateral weakness, facial asymmetry, speech disturbance, visual changes, altered coordination, or altered mental status. Severe headache, vomiting, seizures, or depressed consciousness may raise concern for hemorrhage, but <strong>none of these findings reliably rules in or rules out bleeding.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>EMS cannot reliably distinguish ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke by symptoms, blood pressure, or a stroke scale alone.</strong> Noncontrast head CT is commonly the first imaging test used to identify acute intracranial hemorrhage.</p>\n<h2>BE FAST: recognize the pattern</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>B — Balance:</strong> sudden loss of balance or coordination.</li><li><strong>E — Eyes:</strong> sudden visual loss or other acute visual disturbance.</li><li><strong>F — Face:</strong> new facial droop or asymmetry.</li><li><strong>A — Arms:</strong> new weakness or drift, often on one side.</li><li><strong>S — Speech:</strong> slurred speech, difficulty finding words, or inability to understand language.</li><li><strong>T — Time:</strong> activate the stroke response and document the last-known-well time.</li></ul>\n<p>A negative screening tool does not exclude stroke, especially posterior-circulation stroke, which may present with vertigo, ataxia, visual changes, or other less typical findings.</p>\n<h2>Last known well is not the same as symptom discovery</h2>\n<p><strong>Last known well (LKW)</strong> is the last time the patient was known to be at their neurologic baseline. If a patient wakes up weak at 7 a.m. but was last normal before going to sleep at 10 p.m., the LKW is generally 10 p.m.—not 7 a.m.</p>\n<p>Document the exact time and source of the history, whether deficits are disabling, and any changes during transport. Some patients may qualify for advanced imaging-based treatment selection even when the onset time is uncertain or outside traditional time windows.</p>\n<h2>What changes at the hospital?</h2>\n<p><strong>Ischemic stroke:</strong> eligible patients may receive intravenous thrombolytic therapy after hemorrhage is excluded and other criteria are met. Selected patients with large-vessel occlusion may benefit from <strong>mechanical thrombectomy</strong>, sometimes within extended imaging-selected treatment windows.</p>\n<p><strong>Hemorrhagic stroke:</strong> care focuses on controlling bleeding and preventing secondary injury. Depending on the cause, this may include targeted blood-pressure management, urgent reversal of anticoagulation, neurosurgical or neurointerventional treatment, and management of intracranial pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Thrombolytics are not given for intracranial hemorrhage.</strong> That is one reason rapid imaging matters so much.</p>\n<h2>EMS priorities: what should we actually do?</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Recognize and activate:</strong> use the approved stroke screening tool and alert the receiving facility early.</li><li><strong>Check glucose:</strong> hypoglycemia can mimic stroke; correct it according to protocol without delaying necessary evaluation.</li><li><strong>Establish the timeline:</strong> record LKW, symptom discovery, progression, and witness contact information.</li><li><strong>Support physiology:</strong> monitor airway, oxygenation, ventilation, and circulation. Give oxygen when indicated by hypoxemia rather than automatically to every patient.</li><li><strong>Gather key history:</strong> anticoagulants, antiplatelets, prior stroke, recent surgery or bleeding, seizure activity, and baseline neurologic function.</li><li><strong>Choose destination thoughtfully:</strong> follow regional stroke triage guidance. A suspected large-vessel occlusion may influence routing toward a thrombectomy-capable center when appropriate.</li><li><strong>Avoid harmful delays or assumptions:</strong> do not attempt to diagnose the stroke subtype from blood pressure alone, and do not routinely give aspirin before hemorrhage is excluded. Manage blood pressure according to stroke-specific protocols and medical direction; indiscriminate lowering can compromise perfusion.</li></ol>\n<h2>What about large-vessel occlusion?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>large-vessel occlusion (LVO)</strong> is a blockage of a major cerebral artery. Findings such as gaze deviation, profound unilateral weakness, aphasia, or neglect may increase suspicion. Validated prehospital LVO scales can help with triage, but none is definitive. An LVO is generally an ischemic mechanism, and rapid access to thrombectomy-capable care can be crucial for selected patients.</p>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p><strong>Ischemic stroke is a plumbing blockage. Hemorrhagic stroke is a plumbing leak.</strong> Both can produce sudden neurologic deficits, but treatment differs dramatically. The paramedic's job is not to guess which one it is: recognize stroke, protect the patient, establish an accurate timeline, and move efficiently toward definitive imaging and care.</p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.stroke.org/en/about-stroke/types-of-stroke\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">American Stroke Association: Types of stroke</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/STR.0000000000000407\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHA/ASA 2022 guideline for spontaneous intracerebral hemorrhage</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/STR.0000000000000436\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHA/ASA 2023 guideline for aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for healthcare professionals and students. Follow local stroke protocols and medical direction.</em></p>"},{"title":"Maintenance vs. Resuscitation Fluids: Why 0.9% Saline Is Not Plasma","slug":"maintenance-fluids-vs-resuscitation-fluids-normal-saline-hypertonic-saline","date":"2026-10-29","category":"Clinical Education","description":"Compare maintenance and resuscitation fluids, the chemistry of 0.9% and 3% saline, plasma osmolality, and why chloride load matters.","tags":["IV fluids","normal saline","0.9% saline","3% saline","hypertonic saline","maintenance fluids","resuscitation","chloride","hyperchloremic acidosis"],"html":"<p><strong>Not all IV fluids are intended to do the same job.</strong> Maintenance fluids replace ongoing daily water and electrolyte needs when a patient cannot take enough by mouth. Resuscitation fluids address impaired circulation or perfusion. Choosing a fluid means understanding both <em>why</em> it is being given and <em>what</em> it contains.</p>\n<h2>Maintenance vs. resuscitation: what's the difference?</h2>\n<p><strong>Maintenance fluid</strong> supplies water, electrolytes, and sometimes glucose over time for a patient who cannot meet normal requirements. The amount and composition depend on age, renal function, clinical condition, and losses. Maintenance is not a treatment for shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Resuscitation fluid</strong> is given to improve circulating volume and tissue perfusion when clinically appropriate. In many adult patients who need crystalloid resuscitation, isotonic crystalloids such as lactated Ringer's or Plasma-Lyte are options; 0.9% saline is another option. The cause of shock matters: hemorrhage requires hemorrhage control and early blood products when indicated, rather than unlimited crystalloid.</p>\n<h2>Blood plasma vs. 0.9% normal saline</h2>\n<p>Plasma osmolality is typically about <strong>275–295 mOsm/kg</strong>, often taught as approximately <strong>290–300</strong>. This is not the same measurement as a fluid bag's calculated osmolarity, which is expressed in mOsm/L. They are numerically close for dilute aqueous solutions, but they are not identical units.</p>\n<p><strong>0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline)</strong> contains 0.9 grams of NaCl per 100 mL—equivalent to 9 grams per liter. Each liter contains:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sodium: 154 mEq/L</strong></li><li><strong>Chloride: 154 mEq/L</strong></li><li><strong>Calculated osmolarity: approximately 308 mOsm/L</strong></li></ul>\n<p>Typical plasma sodium is about <strong>135–145 mEq/L</strong>, while plasma chloride is about <strong>98–106 mEq/L</strong>. So even though normal saline is commonly classified as an isotonic crystalloid, it does <strong>not</strong> match the electrolyte composition of plasma. It contains considerably more chloride and has no potassium, calcium, or buffer.</p>\n<h2>Why does the extra chloride matter?</h2>\n<p>Giving large amounts of 0.9% saline can raise serum chloride and reduce the plasma strong ion difference, contributing to <strong>hyperchloremic (normal-anion-gap) metabolic acidosis</strong>. The concern is most relevant with substantial saline exposure; a small amount of saline does not automatically cause clinically important acidosis.</p>\n<p>Balanced crystalloids contain less chloride and include metabolizable anions such as lactate, acetate, or gluconate. These solutions more closely approximate aspects of plasma chemistry, though they are not identical to plasma. Fluid choice still depends on the clinical situation.</p>\n<h2>What about 3% hypertonic saline?</h2>\n<p><strong>3% sodium chloride</strong> contains 3 grams of NaCl per 100 mL—30 grams per liter. Its approximate composition is:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sodium: 513 mEq/L</strong></li><li><strong>Chloride: 513 mEq/L</strong></li><li><strong>Calculated osmolarity: 1,027 mOsm/L</strong></li></ul>\n<p>That makes 3% saline markedly <strong>hypertonic</strong> relative to plasma. It raises extracellular tonicity and draws water from cells into the extracellular compartment. Unlike 0.9% saline, it is <strong>not</strong> a routine maintenance or general-purpose volume-resuscitation fluid.</p>\n<p>Hypertonic saline has specialized indications, including <strong>severe symptomatic hyponatremia</strong> and selected cases of <strong>intracranial hypertension or cerebral edema</strong> under established protocols. It requires careful sodium monitoring and appropriate clinical oversight. Excessively rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can cause osmotic demyelination syndrome.</p>\n<h2>Side-by-side: what is actually in the bag?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>0.9% saline</th><th>3% saline</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NaCl concentration</td><td>9 g/L</td><td>30 g/L</td></tr><tr><td>Sodium</td><td>154 mEq/L</td><td>513 mEq/L</td></tr><tr><td>Chloride</td><td>154 mEq/L</td><td>513 mEq/L</td></tr><tr><td>Calculated osmolarity</td><td>308 mOsm/L</td><td>1,027 mOsm/L</td></tr><tr><td>Common role</td><td>Isotonic crystalloid for selected fluid replacement or resuscitation</td><td>Specialized hypertonic therapy</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Field application: ask three questions</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>What problem am I treating?</strong> Daily fluid needs, volume depletion, distributive shock, hemorrhage, or a neurologic emergency are not interchangeable indications.</li><li><strong>What is in the fluid?</strong> Compare sodium, chloride, osmolarity, and the presence or absence of buffers—not just the bag's name.</li><li><strong>What happens after I give it?</strong> Reassess perfusion, respiratory status, fluid tolerance, and the need for definitive treatment. Large volumes can cause harm, particularly in patients vulnerable to fluid overload.</li></ol>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p><strong>“Normal” saline does not mean “the same as plasma.”</strong> A liter of 0.9% saline delivers 154 mEq of sodium and 154 mEq of chloride; a liter of 3% saline delivers approximately 513 mEq of each. Maintenance fluids support daily requirements, resuscitation fluids address perfusion, and hypertonic saline is a targeted therapy—not a substitute for understanding the patient's physiology.</p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NICE CG174: Intravenous fluid therapy in adults in hospital</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1711584\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SMART trial: Balanced crystalloids versus saline in critically ill adults</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2023/1100/hyponatremia.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AAFP: Diagnosis and management of sodium disorders</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>For healthcare professionals and students. Use local protocols and medical direction for fluid selection and administration.</em></p>"},{"title":"Prolonged QT: When the Heart Takes Too Long to Reset","slug":"prolonged-qt-potassium-channels-pvcs-torsades","date":"2026-10-29","category":"Cardiology & EKG","description":"Understand the ventricular action potential, how IKr-blocking medications delay repolarization, and why a well-timed PVC can trigger torsades de pointes.","tags":["QTc","prolonged QT","potassium channels","IKr","hERG","PVC","torsades de pointes","cardiac action potential"],"html":"<p><strong>A prolonged QT interval means the ventricles are taking longer to electrically recover.</strong> A patient can look stable while delayed repolarization creates an environment in which a premature ventricular beat may initiate a dangerous arrhythmia.</p>\n<h2>First: the ventricular cardiac action potential</h2>\n<p><strong>Phase 0 — rapid depolarization:</strong> Fast sodium channels open and Na⁺ rushes into ventricular muscle cells.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 1 — initial repolarization:</strong> Sodium channels inactivate and a brief outward potassium current lowers the voltage.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 2 — plateau:</strong> Calcium enters through L-type channels while potassium moves outward. These opposing currents sustain the plateau and support contraction.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 — repolarization:</strong> Outward potassium currents, including IKr and IKs, help restore the cell's negative membrane voltage as calcium influx declines. This phase is central to understanding drug-induced QT prolongation.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 4 — resting state:</strong> Potassium conductance helps maintain the resting membrane potential until the next activation.</p>\n<h2>How can medications prolong QT?</h2>\n<p>Some medications inhibit the hERG potassium channel responsible for the rapid delayed rectifier potassium current (<strong>IKr</strong>). This <strong>reduces outward potassium current</strong> during repolarization. Potassium is not completely prevented from leaving the cell; rather, one important route for repolarizing current is impaired.</p>\n<p>The result can be a longer ventricular action potential, delayed repolarization, and a longer QT interval. Certain antiarrhythmics, antipsychotics, antidepressants, antibiotics, and antiemetics are associated with QT prolongation, although their mechanisms and torsades risks differ.</p>\n<h2>What does the QT interval tell us?</h2>\n<p>The QT interval extends from the start of the QRS complex to the end of the T wave, approximating the time required for ventricular depolarization and repolarization. Because QT varies with heart rate, clinicians assess the corrected QT (<strong>QTc</strong>). A QTc of <strong>500 ms or greater</strong> is an important risk marker, not a guarantee that torsades will occur. Correction formulas can be inaccurate at extreme heart rates, and a wide QRS complicates interpretation.</p>\n<h2>Why are PVCs especially concerning?</h2>\n<p>PVCs are often benign, but significant QT prolongation can create uneven electrical recovery across the ventricles. Prolonged action potentials may produce <strong>early afterdepolarizations (EADs)</strong>—abnormal depolarizations occurring before repolarization finishes. An EAD can trigger an extra ventricular beat, sometimes seen as a PVC.</p>\n<p>If a premature impulse arrives when parts of the myocardium have recovered and others remain refractory, it may initiate <strong>torsades de pointes</strong>, a potentially fatal form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.</p>\n<h2>R-on-T and the short–long–short sequence</h2>\n<p>An <strong>R-on-T PVC</strong> occurs when a premature ventricular complex falls on or near the preceding T wave, during ventricular recovery. In a vulnerable heart, this timing can initiate a malignant rhythm. However, not every R-on-T PVC causes torsades, and torsades does not always require a visibly classic R-on-T pattern.</p>\n<p>Acquired long-QT torsades may begin after a <strong>short–long–short</strong> sequence: a premature beat, a pause that further prolongs repolarization, and another early beat that triggers the arrhythmia.</p>\n<h2>What should paramedics look for?</h2>\n<ul><li>Marked QTc prolongation, particularly around or above 500 ms.</li><li>Frequent or pause-dependent PVCs, short–long–short patterns, or runs of polymorphic VT.</li><li>Bradycardia, pauses, syncope, or palpitations.</li><li>Hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and hypocalcemia.</li><li>Recent medication changes, overdose, or combinations of QT-prolonging drugs.</li></ul>\n<p>Low potassium can further reduce repolarization reserve and magnify the risk of medication-associated torsades.</p>\n<h2>If torsades develops</h2>\n<p>Management depends on the patient's condition and rhythm. <strong>IV magnesium sulfate</strong> is a key therapy for torsades associated with prolonged QT, even when measured magnesium is normal. Address contributing medications and correct electrolyte abnormalities under local protocols. Recurrent pause-dependent acquired torsades may require heart-rate acceleration or temporary overdrive pacing with expert direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Sustained polymorphic VT requiring a shock is treated with unsynchronized defibrillation</strong> because reliable synchronization is generally not possible. Pulseless VT/VF requires defibrillation and cardiac arrest care. Not every polymorphic VT involves prolonged QT; treatment differs when QT is normal.</p>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p><strong>Prolonged QT is delayed electrical recovery, not just a long line on an ECG.</strong> Certain drugs reduce outward potassium current, increasing the opportunity for EADs and a premature ventricular beat to trigger torsades. The PVC alone is not the whole problem: the danger is a PVC in an electrically vulnerable myocardium.</p>\n<h2>Further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.109.192704\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHA/ACCF scientific statement: Prevention of torsade de pointes in hospital settings</a></li><li><a href=\"https://crediblemeds.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CredibleMeds: QT-prolonging medications and torsades risk</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for healthcare professionals and students. Follow local protocols and medical direction.</em></p>"},{"title":"Calcium During Massive Transfusion: Why It Matters","slug":"calcium-during-massive-transfusion","date":"2026-10-28","category":"Clinical Education","description":"Citrate in transfused blood can reduce ionized calcium. Learn why that matters for clotting, cardiac function, and hemorrhagic shock.","tags":["calcium","massive transfusion","citrate","hypocalcemia","hemorrhagic shock"],"html":"<p><strong>Blood products are essential in hemorrhagic shock, but rapid transfusion can contribute to hypocalcemia.</strong> Understanding calcium helps first responders recognize another threat during resuscitation.</p><h2>Why calcium matters</h2><p>Ionized calcium is the biologically active fraction of circulating calcium. It supports coagulation, platelet function, myocardial contraction, and vascular tone. Severe hypocalcemia can compound shock and contribute to cardiac electrical instability.</p><h2>How transfusion affects calcium</h2><p>Blood products contain citrate to prevent clotting during storage. Citrate binds ionized calcium after transfusion. The liver normally metabolizes citrate, but rapid transfusion and reduced hepatic perfusion during shock can allow citrate exposure to outpace clearance. Hypocalcemia may also exist before any transfusion in severely injured patients.</p><h2>Why ionized calcium is important</h2><p>Total calcium includes bound forms that are not immediately biologically active. Ionized calcium better reflects calcium available for clotting and cardiovascular function. Its concentration also changes with blood pH. The 2023 European trauma bleeding guideline recommends monitoring and maintaining normal ionized calcium during major trauma and massive transfusion, with correction of hypocalcemia.</p><h2>What can low calcium do?</h2><ul><li>Impair coagulation and platelet-related processes.</li><li>Reduce myocardial contractility and vascular resistance.</li><li>In severe cases, contribute to QT prolongation and dysrhythmias.</li></ul><h2>Calcium replacement requires judgment</h2><p>Calcium chloride and calcium gluconate are not interchangeable at equal volumes: they contain different amounts of elemental calcium. Calcium chloride can cause serious tissue injury with extravasation. Follow approved access, administration, monitoring, and dosing protocols.</p><p>Some hemorrhage-resuscitation protocols use empiric calcium replacement during substantial transfusion, while others emphasize measurement-guided correction. There is no universal prehospital schedule appropriate for every product and system. Although hypocalcemia is associated with worse outcomes, evidence has not established that routine calcium administration itself reduces mortality in massive hemorrhage.</p><h2>What should paramedics remember?</h2><ul><li>Anticipate citrate-associated hypocalcemia when transfusing blood rapidly.</li><li>Monitor ionized calcium when testing is available.</li><li>Consider the full clinical picture when hypotension or instability persists.</li><li>Communicate the number and type of products transfused, any calcium given, and relevant trends.</li><li>Prioritize hemorrhage control and protocol-directed blood resuscitation.</li></ul><h2>The bottom line</h2><p><strong>Blood restores lost components, but citrate can temporarily reduce the calcium needed for clotting and cardiovascular function.</strong> Recognize the risk, monitor when possible, and correct hypocalcemia according to local protocols.</p><h2>Reference</h2><p><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9977110/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">European guideline on management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma, sixth edition (2023), Recommendation 31</a></p>"},{"title":"Hemoglobin After Acute Hemorrhage: Why the First Number Can Mislead You","slug":"hemoglobin-acute-hemorrhage-why-initial-labs-can-mislead","date":"2026-10-28","category":"Clinical Education","description":"Why hemoglobin may initially stay unchanged during major bleeding—and how fluid shifts, kidney water retention, and IV fluids reveal a later decline.","tags":["hemoglobin","hemorrhage","acute blood loss","hemodilution","shock","renal compensation","paramedic"],"html":"<p><strong>A patient can lose significant blood while their first hemoglobin remains nearly unchanged.</strong> Understanding why is essential for anyone assessing acute hemorrhage.</p><h2>Hemoglobin measures concentration, not blood loss</h2><p>Hemoglobin (Hgb), reported in grams per deciliter (g/dL), measures how much hemoglobin is present in a volume of blood. It does not directly measure total circulating blood volume, total red-cell mass, or milliliters lost.</p><p>Imagine removing a portion of well-mixed blood from a container. You remove red cells and plasma together. Less blood remains, but its hemoglobin concentration may initially be similar.</p><h2>What hemoglobin numbers should concern us when blood loss is suspected?</h2><p>Typical adult laboratory reference ranges are approximately <strong>13.8–17.2 g/dL for men</strong> and <strong>12.1–15.1 g/dL for women</strong>, although ranges vary by laboratory and patient factors. Some patients normally run below these ranges, so the <strong>baseline, trend, and clinical presentation</strong> matter more than a single number.</p><p><strong>Hemoglobin around 8–10 g/dL:</strong> Below the usual adult reference range and worth investigating in a patient with suspected bleeding, especially if it represents a new decline. It does not, by itself, establish the volume or acuity of blood loss.</p><p><strong>Hemoglobin below 8 g/dL:</strong> A particularly concerning finding with symptoms, ongoing hemorrhage, or poor perfusion. Evaluate urgently in clinical context.</p><p><strong>Hemoglobin below 7 g/dL:</strong> A common threshold at which red-cell transfusion is considered in <em>hemodynamically stable hospitalized adults</em>, per 2023 AABB guidelines. This threshold <strong>must not be used to delay hemorrhage treatment or transfusion in an unstable, actively bleeding patient</strong>.</p><p><strong>Any hemoglobin with shock findings:</strong> A reading of 12 or 14 g/dL does not exclude life-threatening acute hemorrhage. A rapid decline from a known baseline can also be significant even if the latest value remains within the reference range.</p><h2>Why the first hemoglobin may look normal</h2><p>During acute whole-blood hemorrhage, plasma and red cells leave together. Before significant fluid redistribution or resuscitation, the hemoglobin concentration may change little, even though circulating volume and oxygen delivery are threatened. A normal early result does not exclude major bleeding or shock.</p><h2>Why hemoglobin falls later</h2><p><strong>Fluid shifts:</strong> As vascular volume falls, fluid can move from interstitial spaces into the circulation. This helps refill plasma volume but does not replace lost red cells, so measured hemoglobin can decrease through dilution. This process can begin before substantial kidney-mediated retention.</p><p><strong>Kidney response:</strong> Reduced effective circulating volume activates antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS). The kidneys conserve water and sodium, supporting plasma-volume recovery over time. As fluid is retained without replacing lost red cells, hemoglobin concentration may fall further. Kidney retention is not the only or necessarily the earliest explanation for the decrease.</p><p><strong>IV fluids:</strong> Administered crystalloids can expand plasma volume and dilute hemoglobin. A lower measurement after fluids does not, by itself, establish ongoing bleeding; a stable value does not establish that bleeding has stopped.</p><h2>Why timing matters</h2><p>A patient whose hemoglobin was 12 g/dL before sudden bleeding may have an early sample near that value, followed by a lower result after fluid shifts and resuscitation. This is illustrative, not a predictable timeline. A drop from 9 to 6 g/dL cannot reliably be converted into a specific number of milliliters lost. Body size, baseline anemia, sampling times, fluids, transfusions, and ongoing bleeding all affect the result.</p><h2>What paramedics should take away</h2><ul><li><strong>Treat the patient, not one lab value.</strong> Assess the suspected bleeding source, mental status, perfusion, pulse quality, and vital-sign trends.</li><li><strong>Do not wait for hemoglobin to fall</strong> before escalating suspected life-threatening hemorrhage.</li><li><strong>Communicate timing.</strong> Include when bleeding began, observed blood loss, interventions, fluids, transfusions, and when samples were drawn.</li><li><strong>Interpret serial hemoglobin in context.</strong> Trends are useful alongside clinical findings and the resuscitation timeline.</li><li><strong>Prioritize hemorrhage control</strong> and protocol-directed resuscitation.</li></ul><h2>Capnography: another window into perfusion</h2><p>Hemoglobin measures concentration. <strong>End-tidal carbon dioxide (ETCO₂)</strong> can offer additional information about how effectively blood is delivering carbon dioxide to the lungs. When ventilation and metabolism are relatively stable, a drop in cardiac output or pulmonary blood flow can lower ETCO₂ because less CO₂ reaches ventilated alveoli.</p><p>In hemorrhagic shock, reduced venous return and cardiac output can therefore contribute to <strong>falling ETCO₂</strong>, sometimes while blood pressure is still being maintained. In a 2021 observational study of <em>intubated trauma patients</em>, ETCO₂ below 25 mm Hg was associated with greater odds of hemorrhagic shock on emergency-department arrival. That value is a research finding in a specific population, <strong>not a universal diagnostic cutoff</strong> for all spontaneously breathing patients.</p><p><strong>Interpret the waveform and trend, not just the number.</strong> ETCO₂ is influenced by respiratory rate and tidal volume, airway and sampling issues, dead space, pulmonary embolism, metabolic CO₂ production, and other causes of low cardiac output. Hyperventilation alone can reduce ETCO₂. Capnography cannot quantify blood loss or independently confirm hemorrhage.</p><h2>Putting the pieces together in the field</h2><p>Consider a patient with suspected internal bleeding whose first hemoglobin is 12 g/dL. Their heart rate is increasing, skin is cool, mental status is changing, and ETCO₂ is trending downward despite relatively stable ventilation. <strong>The clinical trajectory warrants concern for worsening perfusion even though the initial hemoglobin appears reassuring.</strong></p><p>Assess the suspected source of hemorrhage, pulse and blood-pressure trends, shock index when appropriate, mental status, skin perfusion, and capillary refill. When available, serial lactate, base deficit, blood gas, and repeat hemoglobin can add context—but none replaces clinical assessment or timely hemorrhage control. For ETCO₂, trends are most interpretable when ventilation is controlled or reasonably consistent.</p><h2>The bottom line</h2><p><strong>Hemoglobin is a concentration, not a blood-volume gauge.</strong> Acute bleeding removes plasma and red cells together, so the initial number can be misleading. Interstitial fluid shifts, renal sodium and water conservation, and IV fluid administration subsequently change plasma volume and reveal dilution. Never allow an initially reassuring hemoglobin to delay care for suspected major hemorrhage.</p><h2>References</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34432752/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bulger et al. Prehospital end-tidal carbon dioxide predicts hemorrhagic shock (2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/annalsats/article/21/7/993/8405873\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Management of Respiratory Failure in Hemorrhagic Shock (2024)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37824153/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AABB red blood cell transfusion guidelines (2023)</a></li></ul><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK537232/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Acute Anemia — NCBI Bookshelf</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK224592/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pathophysiology of Acute Hemorrhagic Shock — NCBI Bookshelf</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1065003/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Clinical Review: Hemorrhagic Shock</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK254/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anemia — Clinical Methods</a></li></ul>"},{"title":"EMTALA: When a Patient Calls 911 From the Emergency Department","slug":"emtala-patient-calls-911-from-emergency-room","date":"2026-10-28","category":"FTO & Education","description":"A practical guide for EMS and ED clinicians: understand screening obligations, patient choice, safe transfers, and how to advocate together when a patient calls 911 from a hospital.","tags":["EMTALA","EMS","emergency department","medical screening examination","MSE","patient advocacy","patient refusal","transfer","nursing","paramedic","FTO"],"html":"<p><strong>A patient calls 911 from inside an emergency department.</strong> Maybe the patient has been waiting, feels worse, is frustrated, or wants care at another hospital. The complaint could be chest pain, abdominal pain, shortness of breath, weakness, or something less obvious. An ambulance arrives. What happens next?</p>\n<p>For the paramedic, this can feel like an unusual 911 call at a facility already equipped to provide emergency care. For the ED nurse or physician, it raises questions about screening, patient choice, continuity of care, and safe disposition. <strong>The goal is not to decide which team is responsible for the inconvenience. It is to make sure the patient is heard, assessed, and not lost between two systems.</strong></p>\n<h2>Start with EMTALA—and what it actually requires</h2>\n<p>The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA) generally requires Medicare-participating hospitals with dedicated emergency departments to offer an <strong>appropriate medical screening examination (MSE)</strong> when an individual comes to the ED seeking examination or treatment for a possible emergency medical condition. If an emergency medical condition (EMC) is found, the hospital must provide stabilizing treatment within its capabilities or arrange an appropriate transfer under the applicable rules.</p>\n<p>An MSE is an evaluation by a physician or another qualified medical professional designated under hospital rules to determine whether an EMC exists. Its scope depends on the presenting symptoms and clinical circumstances. <strong>Registration is not a prerequisite, and triage alone is not automatically an MSE.</strong> A patient may need prompt reassessment even if already triaged.</p>\n<h2>Understand the hospital's perspective</h2>\n<p>ED staff have an obligation to respond to a request for emergency evaluation, assess for an EMC, and follow stabilization and transfer requirements when applicable. They may also be managing multiple high-acuity patients, triage priorities, staffing limitations, and evolving conditions. A wait time by itself does not establish an EMTALA violation, but a change in symptoms or condition deserves attention.</p>\n<p>If a patient wants to leave, hospital staff should distinguish a capable patient's informed refusal from a hospital-arranged transfer. Staff should explain the available evaluation, material risks of leaving, and reasonable alternatives, then document the discussion and the patient's decision. The patient cannot be compelled to accept an examination merely because EMTALA applies.</p>\n<h2>Understand the EMS perspective</h2>\n<p>An independent 911 crew arrives because someone requested emergency assistance. The crew still has duties under its clinical protocols, state law, and medical direction: assess the patient, recognize time-sensitive illness, determine the appropriate response, and document care. <strong>A 911 dispatch does not automatically turn the encounter into an interfacility transfer or guarantee transport to a patient-selected destination.</strong></p>\n<p>EMTALA primarily imposes screening and stabilization duties on covered hospitals. The legal analysis may differ for hospital-owned ambulances, hospital-directed movement, or special circumstances. EMS should not independently declare that the hospital has violated EMTALA; instead, communicate objective concerns, clarify who is directing the disposition, and involve medical control or supervision when necessary.</p>\n<h2>When EMS arrives: a shared approach</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Put the patient first.</strong> EMS begins an appropriate clinical assessment. ED staff promptly respond to new or worsening complaints. If the patient is unstable, both teams prioritize immediate care over arguing about jurisdiction.</li>\n<li><strong>Make direct clinician-to-clinician contact.</strong> The crew should notify the charge nurse or treating clinician, explain why 911 was called, and share concerning findings. Hospital staff should identify a clinical point of contact rather than leaving the crew to negotiate only with registration or security.</li>\n<li><strong>Clarify what care has occurred.</strong> Has triage occurred? Has an MSE been completed by qualified personnel? Has an EMC been identified or ruled out? If the answers are unknown, say so rather than assume.</li>\n<li><strong>Listen to what the patient actually wants.</strong> Is the patient requesting faster evaluation, asking to speak to a clinician, refusing care at this hospital, or requesting transfer? Assess decision-making capacity when refusal is relevant; do not equate frustration with incapacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Explain realistic options.</strong> The ED can offer assessment and address concerns. If the patient chooses to leave, explain that another ED may also have waits and that transport can delay care. EMS destination and transport decisions remain subject to local policy and medical direction.</li>\n<li><strong>Agree on the next step and document it.</strong> Depending on the facts, this may be continued ED evaluation, informed refusal and departure, a properly arranged hospital transfer, or an EMS disposition consistent with applicable rules. Document patient statements, findings, discussions, decisions, and any medical-control consultation.</li></ol>\n<h2>Three situations that must not be confused</h2>\n<h3>Hospital-arranged transfer</h3><p>When a hospital transfers a patient with an unstabilized EMC, EMTALA generally requires safeguards such as treatment to minimize transfer risks, an accepting facility with available capacity, relevant records, appropriate personnel and equipment, and the required certification or documented patient request. Simply calling 911 does not substitute for these requirements.</p>\n<h3>Informed refusal or leaving against medical advice</h3><p>A patient with decision-making capacity may decline offered evaluation or treatment. The hospital should explain the risks and benefits and document the refusal as required. Leaving voluntarily is not automatically a hospital-directed transfer, but neither does a signed form erase obligations that arose before the refusal.</p>\n<h3>Independent 911 request from within the ED</h3><p>The crew should determine who requested transport, whether the hospital is directing it, what screening and treatment have occurred, and what the patient is requesting. The correct outcome depends on the clinical situation, patient capacity, local EMS rules, and hospital responsibilities—not on a blanket claim that EMS must transport or must refuse.</p>\n<h2>How to advocate without creating conflict</h2>\n<p><strong>For EMS:</strong> “We were dispatched because this patient requested help. These are our assessment findings and their stated concerns. Has a medical screening examination been completed, and can we speak with the clinician responsible for the next step?”</p>\n<p><strong>For ED staff:</strong> “Thank you for letting us know. We need to reassess the patient's condition and clarify their wishes. Let's coordinate the safest plan and communicate it directly to the patient.”</p>\n<p>These conversations work best when both teams avoid accusations, avoid making legal conclusions at the bedside, and escalate concerns through clinical leadership or medical control when they cannot agree. Advocacy means making sure the patient receives an appropriate clinical response—not promising a particular destination or outcome.</p>\n<h2>Common misconceptions</h2><ul>\n<li><strong>“They haven't registered, so EMTALA does not apply.”</strong> Registration does not control whether the screening duty has arisen.</li>\n<li><strong>“They were triaged, so the MSE is complete.”</strong> Not necessarily.</li>\n<li><strong>“A long wait proves an EMTALA violation.”</strong> Not by itself; screening appropriateness depends on the circumstances.</li>\n<li><strong>“EMS must transport anyone who calls 911 from the ED.”</strong> Not universally; applicable EMS laws, protocols, and clinical circumstances matter.</li>\n<li><strong>“EMS cannot transport anyone from an ED because of EMTALA.”</strong> Also incorrect; patient choice, hospital involvement, and transfer requirements must be distinguished.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Medic Goats takeaway</h2><blockquote><p><strong>Assess the patient. Hear the concern. Communicate across teams. Clarify the disposition. Advocate for safe care.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<p>The hospital and the ambulance crew may have different legal and operational responsibilities, but they share one clinical priority: <strong>the patient should never be left in the middle.</strong></p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1395dd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">42 U.S.C. § 1395dd — EMTALA statute</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/section-489.24\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">42 CFR § 489.24 — EMTALA regulation</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cms.gov/medicare/regulations-guidance/legislation/emergency-medical-treatment-labor-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CMS — EMTALA guidance</a></li></ul>\n<p><em>Educational discussion only; not legal advice or patient-specific medical direction. Follow local protocols, hospital policies, medical control, and applicable law.</em></p>"},{"title":"Freeze-Dried Plasma: A New Tool for Prehospital Hemorrhagic Shock","slug":"freeze-dried-plasma-prehospital-hemorrhagic-shock","date":"2026-10-28","category":"Sepsis & Shock","description":"How freeze-dried plasma works, the 2026 FDA approval of EZPLAZ, field preparation, transfusion risks, and what the evidence really says about trauma resuscitation.","tags":["freeze-dried plasma","FDP","EZPLAZ","trauma","hemorrhagic shock","prehospital blood","transfusion","damage control resuscitation","whole blood"],"html":"\n<p><strong>What if the plasma a bleeding patient needs could ride on the ambulance shelf instead of waiting in a freezer?</strong> That is the operational promise of freeze-dried plasma (FDP). For EMS clinicians managing life-threatening hemorrhage, the challenge is not simply knowing that plasma helps replace coagulation factors. It is getting a usable blood product to the patient before preventable deterioration occurs.</p>\n<h2>What is freeze-dried plasma?</h2>\n<p>Plasma is the liquid portion of blood. It contains coagulation factors and other proteins needed for hemostasis. Freeze-drying, or lyophilization, removes water from plasma under controlled conditions so the product can be stored in a dry form and reconstituted before transfusion. FDP is still a blood component, not a synthetic clotting drug.</p>\n<p>Traditional frozen plasma requires freezer infrastructure and thawing. FDP reduces those logistical barriers. That matters for ground EMS, air medical teams, rural hospitals, disaster response, and military medicine—especially when the nearest blood bank is far away.</p>\n<h2>Why plasma matters in hemorrhagic shock</h2>\n<p>In uncontrolled bleeding, patients lose circulating volume, oxygen-carrying red blood cells, platelets, and coagulation proteins. Ongoing hemorrhage and trauma-induced coagulopathy can reinforce each other. Plasma replaces multiple coagulation factors and contributes to hemostatic resuscitation; it does <strong>not</strong> provide the oxygen-carrying capacity of red blood cells or whole blood.</p>\n<p>That distinction is critical. Giving plasma alone cannot correct profound anemia or stop an actively bleeding vessel. Early hemorrhage control, appropriate blood-component or whole-blood resuscitation, rapid transport, and definitive surgical or interventional care remain essential.</p>\n<h2>What changed in 2026?</h2>\n<p>On July 29, 2026, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration licensed <strong>EZPLAZ Freeze Dried Plasma</strong>, the first FDA-licensed FDP product in the United States. Its labeled indication is for <strong>adult patients requiring plasma when other plasma products are unavailable</strong>. That is narrower than saying every trauma patient should receive FDP.</p>\n<p>EZPLAZ is manufactured from a single unit of fresh frozen plasma. The FDA describes Group AB and low-titer anti-B Group A products for emergency-use considerations when a recipient's blood type is not yet known. Blood-group compatibility and institutional transfusion policy still matter.</p>\n<h2>How is it prepared in the field?</h2>\n<p>The EZPLAZ kit includes a dry plasma unit, a <strong>250 mL sterile water for injection</strong> bag, a transfer set, and a blood transfusion set. The product is reconstituted according to its approved instructions before transfusion. FDA review documents describe approximately <strong>2.5 minutes</strong> for reconstitution and a <strong>12-month ambient-temperature shelf life</strong> under approved storage conditions.</p>\n<p>These are <em>product-specific</em> details—not universal instructions for every freeze-dried plasma formulation. Clinicians must use the applicable product labeling, aseptic preparation steps, approved administration equipment, and their service's blood-product protocol. Never improvise diluents or preparation steps.</p>\n<h2>FDP versus frozen plasma versus whole blood</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frozen plasma:</strong> supplies coagulation proteins but depends on frozen storage and a thawing process.</li>\n<li><strong>Freeze-dried plasma:</strong> supplies plasma proteins with more flexible storage and point-of-care reconstitution; it still requires a safe transfusion workflow.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-titer group O whole blood:</strong> supplies red blood cells, plasma, and platelets together, with different storage, compatibility, and program requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>FDP is not automatically a substitute for whole blood. The right strategy depends on what products are available, the patient's physiology, the source of hemorrhage, and local medical direction.</p>\n<h2>What does the evidence actually show?</h2>\n<p>A small Australian pilot randomized trial of prehospital FDP for critical traumatic bleeding enrolled 20 patients. It showed that prehospital administration was feasible, but the study was too small to establish a reliable mortality benefit. A 2025 review likewise noted that clinical evidence specific to dried plasma remains limited. We should distinguish the established rationale for early hemostatic resuscitation from proof that one dried-plasma formulation improves survival over another product.</p>\n<p>The FDA's EZPLAZ decision drew on manufacturing and product data, clinical safety information, and experience with related FDP products. Its approval does not mean every ambulance service can immediately stock or administer it; procurement, medical oversight, training, storage, and transfusion systems still need to be established.</p>\n<h2>Risks EMS clinicians must anticipate</h2>\n<p>FDP carries the risks of plasma transfusion, including allergic reactions, hemolytic reactions, transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI), transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO), and infectious risks. Monitor the patient closely, recognize a suspected reaction, stop the transfusion when indicated, and follow the service's reaction and reporting procedures.</p>\n<h2>What this means for prehospital medicine</h2>\n<p>For agencies that cannot practically carry thawed plasma, FDP could expand access to coagulation-factor replacement earlier in the course of severe bleeding. The operational questions are just as important as the pharmacology: Who is eligible? Which product is stocked? How are compatibility and traceability managed? Who prepares it? What is the plan for monitoring, documentation, and handoff?</p>\n<p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> freeze-dried plasma is a potentially important logistics advance in damage-control resuscitation. It makes plasma easier to bring to the patient, but it does not replace hemorrhage control, oxygen-carrying blood products, or definitive care.</p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-licenses-first-ever-freeze-dried-plasma-product-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA: First U.S. freeze-dried plasma license (2026)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/blood-blood-products/ezplaz\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA: EZPLAZ indications and product documents</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/media/194320/download\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">FDA: EZPLAZ scientific review (2026)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37103482/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Prehospital FDP pilot randomized trial (2023)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0887796325000562\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dried Plasma—Where Are We and Where Next? (2025)</a></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"title":"Epinephrine vs. Norepinephrine: Push-Dose Pressors, Drips, and Shock Physiology","slug":"epinephrine-vs-norepinephrine-push-dose-drips-shock","date":"2026-10-28","category":"Sepsis & Shock","description":"Understand alpha and beta receptors, when epinephrine or norepinephrine fits different shock states, and why push-dose pressors are not the same as continuous infusions.","tags":["epinephrine","norepinephrine","push-dose","vasopressors","infusions","septic shock","cardiogenic shock","anaphylaxis","shock","perfusion"],"html":"\n<p><strong>A blood pressure of 72/40 tells you the patient is in trouble. It does not tell you which pressor to choose.</strong> Epinephrine and norepinephrine both stimulate adrenergic receptors, but they do not produce identical effects on the heart, blood vessels, or metabolic markers. Choosing between them starts with identifying why the patient is in shock.</p>\n<h2>Start with the physiology, not the number</h2>\n<p>Mean arterial pressure (MAP) depends broadly on cardiac output (CO) and systemic vascular resistance (SVR): <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> (with central venous pressure omitted for simplicity). Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. A patient can become hypotensive because their vessels are too dilated, their heart cannot pump adequately, circulating volume is insufficient, or several mechanisms coexist.</p>\n<p>Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion, not merely a low blood pressure. Track mental status, skin perfusion, urine output when available, serial pressures, pulse quality, and other relevant findings. A better MAP does not automatically mean better microcirculatory flow.</p>\n<h2>Alpha and beta: what the receptors actually do</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Alpha-1 (α1):</strong> constricts arterial and venous vessels, increasing vascular tone and often blood pressure. Excessive constriction may impair tissue perfusion.</li><li><strong>Beta-1 (β1):</strong> increases myocardial contractility and usually heart rate, potentially increasing cardiac output—but also myocardial oxygen demand and arrhythmia risk.</li><li><strong>Beta-2 (β2):</strong> relaxes bronchial smooth muscle and causes vasodilation in some vascular beds; it also promotes metabolic effects, including increased lactate production.</li></ul>\n<h2>Epinephrine: the heart and the vessels</h2>\n<p>Epinephrine stimulates <strong>α1, β1, and β2</strong> receptors. The relative effect varies with dose and patient physiology: beta-mediated cardiac stimulation and bronchodilation are prominent, while alpha-mediated vasoconstriction becomes particularly important at higher exposure. Epinephrine can increase heart rate, contractility, and vascular tone.</p>\n<p>That makes epinephrine especially important in <strong>anaphylaxis</strong>, where bronchospasm, vasodilation, and capillary leak may occur together. Intramuscular epinephrine is first-line treatment for anaphylaxis; an IV infusion may be considered in refractory cases by appropriately trained clinicians under protocol and close monitoring. IV push-dose epinephrine is <em>not</em> a substitute for prompt IM treatment of routine anaphylaxis.</p>\n<p>In shock, epinephrine may be used as an additional vasopressor when initial therapy is inadequate, or in selected circumstances involving poor cardiac output. It can provoke tachycardia, dysrhythmias, increased myocardial oxygen demand, hyperglycemia, and elevated lactate. Importantly, an epinephrine-associated lactate rise can reflect β2-driven aerobic glycolysis and does not always mean worsening tissue hypoxia; interpret the trend in clinical context.</p>\n<h2>Norepinephrine: restoring vascular tone</h2>\n<p>Norepinephrine acts strongly at <strong>α1</strong> receptors and also has <strong>β1</strong> activity, with relatively little β2 effect. It raises vascular tone and can support blood pressure without the same degree of direct chronotropic stimulation typically seen with epinephrine. Heart-rate responses vary; reflex slowing can offset direct β1 stimulation.</p>\n<p>In adult <strong>septic shock</strong>, current major guidelines recommend norepinephrine as the <strong>first-line vasopressor</strong>. When vasodilation persists despite appropriate resuscitation, increasing vascular tone addresses an important part of the underlying physiology. A commonly recommended initial MAP target is <strong>65 mm Hg</strong>, individualized to the patient and response. Norepinephrine does not treat the infection: antimicrobials, source control, and reassessed fluid resuscitation remain essential.</p>\n<h2>Push-dose versus a continuous drip</h2>\n<p><strong>Push-dose vasopressors</strong> are small, intermittent IV boluses used by appropriately trained clinicians as temporary support during abrupt hypotension—for example, around induction or while preparing an infusion. Their effect can be brief and variable, and repeated boluses may produce blood-pressure swings. Evidence supporting routine push-dose use in prehospital care is limited, and medication preparation errors are a major concern.</p>\n<p><strong>Continuous infusions</strong> deliver medication at a controlled rate and allow titration to a defined perfusion goal. They are generally better suited to ongoing vasopressor-dependent shock. Use an infusion pump, a clearly labeled standardized concentration, reliable vascular access, frequent reassessment, and continuous ECG and blood-pressure monitoring according to local protocol. Peripheral norepinephrine can be started through a suitable proximal IV when delaying vasopressors for central access would be harmful, with close site surveillance and an extravasation plan.</p>\n<p><strong>Medication safety:</strong> Push-dose epinephrine, infusion epinephrine, and norepinephrine products may come in very different concentrations. Confusing concentrations, units, or routes can cause catastrophic overdose. This article intentionally does not provide a universal bedside mixing recipe or bolus dose. Use your agency's approved concentrations, dosing charts, pump library, and medical direction; independently verify the drug, concentration, route, and dose.</p>\n<h2>Which agent fits which shock state?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Shock pattern</th><th>Physiology and practical considerations</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Septic/distributive</strong></td><td>Vascular tone is often profoundly reduced. Norepinephrine is generally first-line after or alongside individualized fluid resuscitation; additional agents depend on response and protocol.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Anaphylactic</strong></td><td>Vasodilation, capillary leak, and bronchospasm can coexist. IM epinephrine is first-line. Refractory shock may require an epinephrine infusion and expert escalation.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Cardiogenic</strong></td><td>Pump failure limits forward flow. Norepinephrine may support severe hypotension, but increasing afterload can also burden the failing heart. An inotrope and definitive cause-directed treatment may be needed. Epinephrine is not automatically the best choice.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hypovolemic/hemorrhagic</strong></td><td>The primary problem is inadequate circulating volume. Control bleeding and restore appropriate volume/blood products; pressors do not replace hemorrhage control and may worsen perfusion if used instead of resuscitation.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Neurogenic</strong></td><td>Loss of sympathetic tone produces vasodilation, sometimes with bradycardia. Norepinephrine is commonly considered because of its vascular and cardiac receptor activity, alongside spinal injury management and protocol-specific targets.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Obstructive</strong></td><td>Conditions such as tension pneumothorax, tamponade, or massive pulmonary embolism impede circulation. Pressors may temporize, but the obstruction requires urgent definitive treatment.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Field scenario: two patients with the same MAP</h2>\n<p><strong>Patient A:</strong> Fever, altered mentation, warm extremities, suspected infection, and persistent hypotension after appropriately reassessed fluids. Vasodilatory septic shock is likely. Norepinephrine aligns with guideline-based first-line vasopressor therapy while definitive sepsis care continues.</p>\n<p><strong>Patient B:</strong> Hypotension, diffuse wheezing, urticaria, and facial swelling minutes after a medication exposure. This is anaphylaxis until proven otherwise. <strong>Immediate IM epinephrine</strong> is the priority—not waiting for a norepinephrine drip or relying on an IV push-dose strategy.</p>\n<p>The monitor may display the same pressure in both patients. The physiology—and therefore the treatment priorities—are different.</p>\n<h2>What about the peri-intubation crash?</h2>\n<p>Sedation and positive-pressure ventilation can reduce sympathetic tone and venous return. Anticipate hemodynamic collapse in a patient with poor reserve. Optimize the underlying cause, reassess fluid responsiveness, prepare an appropriate infusion when indicated, and follow the agency's protocol for any rescue push-dose vasopressor. Avoid treating repeated boluses as a substitute for a sustained plan.</p>\n<h2>What I want you to remember</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Identify the mechanism of shock.</strong> Is the dominant problem vascular tone, cardiac output, volume, or obstruction?</li><li><strong>Norepinephrine is generally first-line in adult septic shock.</strong> Epinephrine is essential in anaphylaxis and has selected roles in other shock states.</li><li><strong>Push-dose is a bridge, not a destination.</strong> Ongoing shock needs reassessment, definitive treatment, and often a titratable infusion.</li><li><strong>Never chase MAP alone.</strong> Recheck end-organ perfusion and watch for ischemia, arrhythmias, and extravasation.</li><li><strong>Medication preparation is a critical safety step.</strong> Use approved concentrations and protocols rather than improvised dilution instructions.</li></ol>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Pressors can improve the pressure. Your job is to improve the perfusion—and treat why it failed.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.sccm.org/clinical-resources/guidelines/guidelines/surviving-sepsis-campaign-international-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines for Management of Sepsis and Septic Shock (2021)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18654759/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Myburgh et al. — Epinephrine compared with norepinephrine in critically ill patients with shock (2008)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29856780/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Levy et al. — Epinephrine versus norepinephrine in cardiogenic shock after acute myocardial infarction (2018)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.resus.org.uk/library/additional-guidance/guidance-anaphylaxis/emergency-treatment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Resuscitation Council UK: Emergency Treatment of Anaphylaxis</a></li></ul>\n"},{"title":"Posterior Circulation Stroke: Why We Miss the Stroke That Doesn't Look Like a Stroke","slug":"posterior-circulation-stroke-why-it-gets-missed","date":"2026-10-27","category":"Neurology & Stroke","description":"Why posterior circulation strokes are frequently missed, how they can present with vertigo and imbalance rather than classic FAST signs, and what EMS clinicians should assess.","tags":["posterior stroke","posterior circulation","stroke","neurology","vertigo","dizziness","ataxia","BE-FAST","cerebellum","brainstem","basilar artery","EMS"],"html":"\n<p><strong>A patient suddenly becomes dizzy, vomits, and cannot walk without assistance. Their face is symmetric, speech sounds normal, and both arms stay up. Is that a negative stroke assessment—or a stroke we are about to miss?</strong></p>\n<p>Posterior circulation strokes are a major diagnostic blind spot in emergency medicine and prehospital care. They can look less like the classic stroke we are trained to recognize and more like vertigo, dehydration, a stomach illness, or a benign inner-ear problem. The absence of facial droop or unilateral arm weakness does <strong>not</strong> exclude stroke.</p>\n<h2>What is a posterior circulation stroke?</h2>\n<p>The vertebral and basilar arteries supply much of the brainstem, cerebellum, occipital lobes, and portions of the thalamus. Injury here can disrupt balance, coordination, eye movements, vision, swallowing, breathing, and consciousness. The resulting symptoms may be dramatic, subtle, or fluctuate over time.</p>\n<p>Posterior circulation events account for roughly <strong>20–25% of ischemic strokes</strong>. A 2026 review reports that they are approximately <strong>three times more likely to be initially missed</strong> in emergency care than anterior circulation strokes; estimates vary across studies and settings. The central message is consistent: these strokes are disproportionately overlooked.</p>\n<h2>Why the usual stroke picture can fail us</h2>\n<p>FAST emphasizes facial droop, arm weakness, and speech changes. Those findings are important, but a patient with a cerebellar or brainstem stroke may have none of them. BE-FAST adds <strong>Balance</strong> and <strong>Eyes</strong>, which prompts us to look for sudden gait instability, loss of coordination, double vision, or other acute visual changes. Even BE-FAST is a screening aid—not a rule-out test.</p>\n<p>The NIH Stroke Scale can also underestimate posterior circulation injury. A patient may score relatively low despite disabling vertigo, severe truncal ataxia, diplopia, or a dangerous vertebrobasilar occlusion. <strong>A low NIHSS does not mean a posterior stroke is harmless.</strong></p>\n<h2>Red flags that deserve a second look</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sudden, persistent dizziness or vertigo</strong>, especially with other neurological findings.</li>\n<li><strong>New inability to stand, sit upright, or walk independently</strong> because of severe imbalance or truncal ataxia.</li>\n<li><strong>Double vision, new visual field loss, or abnormal eye movements.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Difficulty swallowing, slurred speech, or a new hoarse voice.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>One-sided incoordination</strong> or unusual crossed findings involving the face and body.</li>\n<li><strong>New severe headache or neck pain</strong>, particularly when vertebral artery dissection is a concern.</li>\n<li><strong>Decreased alertness or rapidly worsening neurological status</strong>, especially concerning for basilar artery involvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No single symptom proves a stroke. Most patients with dizziness do not have one. The challenge is recognizing a <strong>sudden, concerning pattern</strong> rather than labeling all dizziness as peripheral vertigo.</p>\n<h2>The patient who passes FAST but cannot walk</h2>\n<p>Imagine a 58-year-old who suddenly develops continuous spinning dizziness, repeated vomiting, and an inability to stand without falling. Their face is symmetric. They move both arms equally. Their speech is clear. A routine FAST screen may be negative.</p>\n<p>What changes the assessment? The <strong>abrupt onset</strong>, persistent symptoms, and profound new gait or truncal instability. Those findings should trigger concern for a central cause, including cerebellar stroke, even without classic weakness. Document the onset or last-known-well time and the specific functional deficit: “Unable to sit unsupported” communicates more than “complains of dizziness.”</p>\n<h2>Why early testing may offer false reassurance</h2>\n<p>A noncontrast head CT is essential in many stroke pathways to assess for hemorrhage, but it is relatively insensitive for early ischemia in the posterior fossa. Even early diffusion-weighted MRI can occasionally miss a small or very early posterior circulation infarct. A normal early scan therefore does not automatically erase a strongly concerning clinical presentation.</p>\n<p>Specialized bedside examinations such as <strong>HINTS</strong> (head impulse, nystagmus, test of skew) can help trained clinicians evaluate an appropriate <em>acute vestibular syndrome</em>—typically continuous dizziness with ongoing spontaneous nystagmus. HINTS is <strong>not</strong> a universal dizziness test, is not intended for brief episodic or positional dizziness, and should not be used by untrained providers to declare a patient stroke-free.</p>\n<h2>What should we do differently in the field?</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start with timing.</strong> Was onset sudden? Are symptoms continuous or episodic? What was the last-known-well time?</li>\n<li><strong>Use the stroke screen, then go beyond it.</strong> Assess balance, gait when safe, limb coordination, vision, speech, swallowing complaints, and other focal findings.</li>\n<li><strong>Take inability to walk seriously.</strong> Do not dismiss severe new ataxia simply because grip strength and facial symmetry are normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Check glucose and assess for mimics</strong> without allowing a plausible alternative to prematurely end the stroke assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Activate and communicate per local protocol.</strong> Describe the posterior circulation concern and the actual findings; use appropriate stroke destination and prearrival notification procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Reassess frequently.</strong> Posterior circulation symptoms can fluctuate or deteriorate; protect the airway and support ventilation or circulation when clinically necessary.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The takeaway</h2>\n<blockquote><strong>A negative FAST screen is not the same as a negative stroke assessment.</strong></blockquote>\n<p>Posterior circulation stroke is easy to miss precisely because the patient may not look like our mental picture of stroke. When a patient suddenly loses balance, develops disabling vertigo, or has new eye movement or coordination abnormalities, keep the brainstem and cerebellum in the differential. The goal is not to call every dizzy patient a stroke. It is to avoid overlooking the patient whose stroke does not announce itself with a facial droop.</p>\n<h2>Research and further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/neurology/articles/10.3389/fneur.2026.1960712/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Seetge &amp; Tarnutzer (2026): Diagnostic challenges in posterior circulation ischemic stroke presenting with acute dizziness and vertigo</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://psnet.ahrq.gov/web-mm/neurological-red-flags-missed-stroke-after-intermittent-episodes-dizziness-and-headache\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ PSNet (2024): Neurological red flags—A missed stroke after intermittent dizziness and headache</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37806810/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Pearls for the Emergency Clinician: Posterior Circulation Stroke (2023)</a>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41450389/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Accuracy of First-Line Tests for Posterior Circulation Stroke (2025 scoping review)</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><small>Educational content for EMS clinicians. Follow your agency's stroke assessment, activation, and destination protocols.</small></p>\n"},{"title":"EKG Concordance vs. Discordance: When ST Changes Are a Warning Sign","slug":"ekg-concordance-discordance-sgarbossa","html":"\n<p><strong>When the QRS is wide, the ST segment can look abnormal even when there is no acute coronary occlusion. The key is understanding which direction the ST segment is supposed to go.</strong></p>\n<p>Concordance and discordance are two of the most useful concepts for interpreting an ECG with left bundle branch block (LBBB) or a ventricular paced rhythm. They help us distinguish expected repolarization changes from findings that should raise concern for acute myocardial infarction.</p>\n<h2>First, look at the QRS</h2>\n<p>Find the dominant direction of the QRS complex in the lead you're examining. Is it mostly upright (positive) or mostly downward (negative)? Then compare the ST segment's displacement from the baseline to that direction.</p>\n<h3>Concordance: moving in the same direction</h3>\n<p><strong>Concordant ST elevation</strong> means the ST segment is elevated in a lead with a predominantly positive QRS. <strong>Concordant ST depression</strong> means the ST segment is depressed in a lead with a predominantly negative QRS. The ST deviation and dominant QRS deflection point in the same direction.</p>\n<h3>Discordance: moving in opposite directions</h3>\n<p><strong>Discordant ST elevation</strong> means the ST segment is elevated when the dominant QRS is negative. <strong>Discordant ST depression</strong> means the ST segment is depressed when the dominant QRS is positive.</p>\n<p>In LBBB and ventricular pacing, some discordant ST-T changes are expected. This is called <em>appropriate discordance</em>: altered ventricular activation also changes the direction of repolarization. The presence of discordance alone is not a diagnosis of infarction.</p>\n<h2>Why it matters in LBBB and paced rhythms</h2>\n<p>In a typical narrow-QRS ECG, ST elevation may be easier to recognize. With LBBB or ventricular pacing, the abnormal sequence of ventricular activation creates secondary ST-T changes that can mask an acute coronary occlusion—or imitate one.</p>\n<p>The question becomes: <strong>Is the ST change appropriate for the size and direction of the QRS, or is it out of proportion?</strong></p>\n<h2>The original Sgarbossa criteria</h2>\n<p>These criteria were developed to help identify acute myocardial infarction in the setting of LBBB. The classic findings are:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Concordant ST elevation of at least 1 mm</strong> in any lead with a positive QRS (5 points).</li>\n<li><strong>Concordant ST depression of at least 1 mm in V1–V3</strong> (3 points). In these leads, the QRS is commonly predominantly negative in LBBB.</li>\n<li><strong>Discordant ST elevation of at least 5 mm</strong> in a lead with a negative QRS (2 points).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A classic score of 3 or more is relatively specific but not sensitive: <strong>absence of the criteria does not rule out an acute occlusion.</strong> The fixed 5 mm discordant-elevation rule also has an important weakness. Five millimeters may be excessive for a small QRS but expected with a very deep S wave.</p>\n<h2>Modified Sgarbossa: think proportion, not just millimeters</h2>\n<p>The Smith-modified Sgarbossa criteria keep the first two findings and replace the third with a proportional rule:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Concordant ST elevation of at least 1 mm in a lead with a positive QRS.</li>\n<li>Concordant ST depression of at least 1 mm in V1–V3.</li>\n<li>Discordant ST elevation of at least 1 mm where the ST elevation is <strong>at least 25% of the depth of the preceding S wave</strong> (ST elevation / |S-wave depth| ≥ 0.25).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, if the S wave is 8 mm deep and the ST segment is elevated 3 mm, the ratio is 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375, or 37.5%. That is disproportionately discordant and meets the proportional criterion. If the S wave is 20 mm deep and the ST elevation is 3 mm, the ratio is 15% and does not meet that criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> The proportional rule applies to discordant <em>ST elevation</em> in leads with a predominantly negative QRS. It is not a general rule for every ST depression or T-wave inversion.</p>\n<h2>A field example</h2>\n<p>You're assessing a 68-year-old patient with diaphoresis, persistent chest pressure, and a known LBBB. The ECG shows a predominantly negative QRS in V3, with an S wave 10 mm deep and 3 mm of ST elevation.</p>\n<p>Three millimeters of elevation may sound modest, but 3 ÷ 10 = 30%. This meets the modified proportional discordance criterion and should heighten concern for acute coronary occlusion in this clinical context. It warrants prompt escalation according to local STEMI/occlusion-MI pathways and medical direction.</p>\n<p>Now imagine the same 3 mm of ST elevation with a 25 mm-deep S wave. That is only 12%. It does not meet the proportional criterion—but a negative criterion <strong>does not make an unstable or ischemic patient safe</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Concordance and discordance are not exclusive to LBBB</h2>\n<p>These terms describe the relationship between the dominant QRS deflection and ST-segment deviation in an individual lead. <strong>Concordant</strong> means the ST deviation points in the same direction as the dominant QRS; <strong>discordant</strong> means they point in opposite directions. You can describe this relationship in LBBB, right bundle branch block (RBBB), and paced rhythms. What changes is <em>which ST-T patterns are expected</em> and which interpretation tools have been validated.</p>\n<h2>What appropriate discordance looks like in RBBB</h2>\n<p>In typical RBBB, the right ventricle activates later than the left. This creates a terminal positive R′ deflection in the right precordial leads, commonly V1–V2 (sometimes extending into V3). The resulting secondary repolarization changes often include <strong>ST depression and T-wave inversion opposite that terminal positive deflection</strong>.</p>\n<p>That means ST depression and inverted T waves in V1–V3 may be expected with RBBB; they are not automatically proof of ischemia. Unlike LBBB, the left ventricle's activation is relatively preserved in RBBB, so conventional ST-elevation patterns are generally easier to evaluate.</p>\n<h3>When RBBB should make you look closer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New or concerning ST elevation:</strong> Do not explain away ischemic ST elevation merely because RBBB is present. Evaluate the lead distribution, magnitude, reciprocal changes, and clinical picture.</li>\n<li><strong>ST depression outside the expected pattern:</strong> Changes in additional leads, dynamic changes, or a distribution inconsistent with uncomplicated RBBB may raise concern.</li>\n<li><strong>Clinical instability:</strong> Persistent ischemic symptoms, shock, or concerning serial ECG changes require escalation even without classic ST-elevation criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>LBBB versus RBBB: the practical difference</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>LBBB</th><th>RBBB</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Are secondary ST-T changes expected?</td><td>Yes, often across multiple leads</td><td>Yes, especially in right precordial leads</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Is discordance automatically abnormal?</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Are standard ST-elevation patterns harder to interpret?</td><td>Often substantially</td><td>Usually less so</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Are modified Sgarbossa criteria routinely used?</td><td>Yes, in the appropriate clinical setting</td><td>No; not validated as a general RBBB rule</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Two patients, two interpretations</h2>\n<p><strong>Patient A — LBBB:</strong> A patient with ongoing chest pressure has a predominantly negative QRS in V3, an S wave 10 mm deep, and 3 mm of ST elevation. The elevation is 30% of S-wave depth. This meets the modified Sgarbossa proportional criterion and raises concern for acute coronary occlusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Patient B — RBBB:</strong> A patient with diaphoresis and chest pressure has RBBB with new ST elevation in V2–V4 and reciprocal changes. Do not label that ST elevation an expected RBBB finding. Assess it as potentially ischemic, compare with previous ECGs, repeat the tracing when indicated, and escalate according to protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>One important limitation:</strong> Sgarbossa and modified Sgarbossa were developed for LBBB-type conduction patterns and have evidence in ventricular paced rhythms. They should not simply be transferred to RBBB. Neither a negative Sgarbossa assessment nor an ECG without classic STEMI criteria rules out an acute coronary occlusion.</p>\n<h2>What I want you to remember in the field</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Start with the QRS.</strong> Decide whether the dominant deflection is positive or negative before labeling ST deviation concordant or discordant.</li>\n<li><strong>Discordance can be normal.</strong> In LBBB, RBBB, and ventricular pacing, some opposite-direction ST-T changes may be expected; the pattern differs by conduction abnormality.</li>\n<li><strong>Concordance needs context.</strong> In LBBB or ventricular pacing, concordant ST deviation meeting Sgarbossa criteria is concerning; do not apply that rule indiscriminately to RBBB.</li>\n<li><strong>Proportion matters.</strong> Excessive discordant ST elevation relative to S-wave depth may reveal an acute occlusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Never use a negative ECG rule to dismiss a sick patient.</strong> Repeat the ECG when appropriate, review prior tracings, assess for ongoing ischemia, and follow local consultation and activation protocols.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Don't ask only, “Is the ST segment elevated?” Ask, “Is that ST segment behaving the way this QRS should make it behave?”</strong></p></blockquote>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Sgarbossa+electrocardiographic+diagnosis+evolving+acute+myocardial+infarction+left+bundle+branch+block+1996\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sgarbossa et al. — Original criteria, NEJM (1996)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Smith+modified+Sgarbossa+ST+elevation+S-wave+ratio+2012\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Smith et al. — Proportional ST-elevation criterion (2012)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=modified+Sgarbossa+criteria+ventricular+paced+rhythm+2021\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Modified Sgarbossa criteria in ventricular paced rhythms</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for EMS clinicians. ECG criteria support—but do not replace—clinical assessment, serial ECG interpretation, local protocols, and medical direction.</em></p>","date":"2026-10-27","category":"Cardiology & EKG"},{"title":"Resuscitate Before You Intubate: Preventing Peri-Intubation Hypotension","slug":"resuscitate-before-you-intubate-preventing-peri-intubation-hypotension","html":"<div class=\"sqs-html-content\" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The tube may save the patient's airway—but the process of getting the tube in can destabilize everything else.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">When critically ill patients require emergency intubation, we often focus heavily on oxygenation, positioning, equipment, and first-pass success. All of those things matter.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But there is another threat that deserves just as much attention:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Hemodynamic collapse.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Peri-intubation hypotension and cardiovascular collapse are common in critically ill patients and are associated with worse outcomes. Recent literature continues to identify hemodynamic instability as one of the major complications surrounding emergency intubation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The key mindset is simple:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Don't just prepare to intubate the patient. Prepare the patient to survive the intubation.</strong></p></blockquote><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Why Does Blood Pressure Drop After Intubation?</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Critically ill patients may already be operating with very little physiologic reserve.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Then RSI introduces several additional stressors:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Induction medications can decrease sympathetic tone and vascular tone.</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Positive-pressure ventilation can reduce venous return.</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Apnea removes the patient's ability to compensate through spontaneous ventilation.</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Paralysis eliminates muscle tone that may have been helping maintain venous return.</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The underlying cause of shock is still present.</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Put all of that together and a patient who was barely maintaining their blood pressure before RSI may become profoundly hypotensive—or arrest—after induction and intubation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This is why <strong>\"What is their blood pressure right now?\"</strong> should be only one part of the question.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A better question is:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"How much cardiovascular reserve does this patient have?\"</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Before You Push the Medications</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Take a moment to optimize what you can.</p><h3 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">1. Establish reliable vascular access</h3><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Have dependable IV access before RSI whenever possible. If peripheral access is difficult or time is critical, IO access can provide a rapid alternative.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You don't want to discover that you have no reliable route for fluids or vasoactive medications after induction.</p><h3 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">2. Treat the shock—not just the number</h3><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Fluids may be appropriate in selected patients, particularly when hypovolemia is contributing to the instability.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But <strong>more fluid is not automatically better.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Think about the cause of the hypotension:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Hemorrhage?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Sepsis?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Cardiogenic shock?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Obstructive shock?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Right ventricular failure?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The intervention should match the physiology.</p><h3 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">3. Have vasopressor support ready</h3><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If the patient is already hypotensive or clearly at risk for cardiovascular collapse, consider having vasopressor therapy prepared <strong>before induction</strong> according to your local protocol.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This may mean starting or increasing a continuous vasopressor infusion when appropriate rather than waiting until after the blood pressure crashes.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Push-dose vasopressors are sometimes used as a bridge, but current evidence does <strong>not</strong> establish them as a substitute for treating the underlying shock or using an appropriate continuous infusion when indicated.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Choose Your Induction Strategy Carefully</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">One of the most important principles is:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The induction dose should reflect the patient's physiology.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A severely shocked patient may not tolerate the same induction strategy or dose that a relatively stable patient would.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This does <strong>not</strong> mean automatically choosing one induction medication for every critically ill patient.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Recent evidence comparing ketamine and etomidate found similar mortality, while ketamine was associated with more cardiovascular collapse and post-induction hypotension than etomidate in the analyzed trials.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The takeaway isn't \"ketamine is bad\" or \"etomidate is always better.\"</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The takeaway is:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Know your induction agent, understand its hemodynamic effects, and dose according to the patient's physiology and your protocol.</strong></p></blockquote><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't Forget the Ventilator</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The danger doesn't end when the tube passes the cords.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Once positive-pressure ventilation begins, venous return can decrease and intrathoracic pressure can rise.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This is particularly important in patients who are:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Hypovolemic</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Vasodilated</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Right-heart dependent</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Obstructive</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Severely acidotic</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Already dependent on spontaneous respiratory effort</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A patient who tolerated spontaneous breathing may deteriorate rapidly once you take over their ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The ventilator is part of the resuscitation—not an afterthought.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A Better RSI Question</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Instead of asking:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"Is this patient ready to be intubated?\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Ask:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"Is this patient optimized enough to tolerate intubation?\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">That doesn't mean delaying a lifesaving airway indefinitely.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">It means using the time available to correct reversible threats while preparing for the physiologic consequences of RSI.</p><h3 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Before Induction, Think:</h3><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>AIRWAY</strong></p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Do I have my primary and backup airway plans?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is my equipment ready?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Who is doing what?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>OXYGENATION</strong></p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is the patient adequately preoxygenated?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How much apnea can they tolerate?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>CIRCULATION</strong></p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">What's causing the hypotension?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Do I need fluid, blood, or vasopressor support?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Do I have reliable vascular access?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>MEDICATIONS</strong></p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is my induction dose appropriate for this patient's physiology?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is my paralytic ready?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is post-intubation sedation ready?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>POST-INTUBATION</strong></p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">What happens immediately after the tube goes in?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">What is my ventilation strategy?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">What will I do if the blood pressure suddenly drops?</p></li></ul><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The Bottom Line</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Intubation is not simply an airway procedure. It is a physiologic stress test.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Critically ill patients can deteriorate because of the combination of induction, apnea, positive-pressure ventilation, paralysis, and their underlying disease.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The goal isn't simply:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"Get the tube.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The goal is:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"Get the tube while keeping the patient alive and physiologically stable.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">So before pushing the induction medication, take a moment to ask:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"What happens to this patient's blood pressure when I take away their compensatory mechanisms?\"</strong></p></blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If you have an answer—and a plan—you are much better prepared for the physiologically difficult airway.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Resuscitate. Optimize. Intubate. Then immediately reassess.</strong></p></div>","date":"2019-05-28 15:13:10","category":"EMS Education"},{"title":"Be the FTO You Needed: Making Field Training Better","slug":"be-the-fto-you-needed-making-field-training-better","html":"<div class=\"sqs-html-content\" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Every paramedic remembers their first day in the field.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You probably remember the nerves.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The questions.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The fear of making a mistake.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And, most importantly, you probably remember how your FTO made you feel.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A good FTO can help build a confident, competent provider. A poor FTO can make a new provider question whether they belong in EMS at all.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Field training isn't just about teaching protocols.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>It's about developing clinicians.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Start With Communication</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't wait until problems develop to talk about expectations.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">On day one, have a conversation about:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Learning style</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Expectations</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Communication</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Feedback</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Safety</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Clinical decision-making</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Documentation</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Professionalism</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Tell your trainee what you expect—and ask what they expect from you.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A trainee shouldn't have to spend their entire field training period trying to figure out what their FTO wants.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Clear expectations create a better learning environment.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Let Them Learn</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">One of the hardest things for an FTO to do is <strong>step back.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You've been on thousands of calls.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You know the protocol.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You know what the patient needs.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You can probably anticipate the treatment plan before the trainee finishes their assessment.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But if you constantly take over, your trainee never gets the opportunity to develop their own clinical judgment.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Give them room to:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Assess.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Think.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Make decisions.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Communicate.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Treat.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Of course, patient safety comes first.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If the trainee is about to make an unsafe decision or the patient's condition requires immediate intervention, step in.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But don't confuse <strong>\"they didn't do it my way\"</strong> with <strong>\"they did it wrong.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">There is often more than one safe and effective way to accomplish the same goal.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't Micromanage</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Not every mistake needs to become a major teaching moment.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Sometimes the trainee simply needs to experience the call.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Constantly correcting small things can create anxiety and cause the trainee to focus more on <strong>pleasing the FTO</strong> than taking care of the patient.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Instead, identify the things that truly matter:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Patient safety.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Clinical judgment.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Communication.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Assessment.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Treatment.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Professionalism.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Save the small stuff for the appropriate teaching moment.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Remember: You're Teaching More Than Medicine</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Your trainee is watching you—even when you don't realize it.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">They're watching:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you speak to patients</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you treat family members</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you interact with nurses and physicians</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you communicate with dispatch</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you handle difficult calls</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you respond when you're wrong</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How you handle stress</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">How seriously you take patient care</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You're not just teaching them how to practice EMS.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>You're showing them what kind of provider to become.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If you're disrespectful, careless, arrogant, or dismissive, they may learn that behavior too.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Be the example you want them to follow.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You Don't Have to Know Everything</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Your trainee will eventually ask you a question you don't know the answer to.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">When that happens, don't make something up.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Say:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"I don't know. Let's find out.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">There's nothing wrong with admitting you don't know something.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">In fact, it demonstrates something incredibly important:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Good clinicians never stop learning.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Your trainee doesn't need an FTO who knows everything.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">They need an FTO who knows how to think, how to find answers, and how to keep learning.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Give Feedback That Actually Helps</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">\"That was a terrible call.\"</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>That's not feedback.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">It's criticism.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Good feedback tells the trainee:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What happened.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Why it mattered.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What could be done differently.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>How to improve next time.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Instead of:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">\"You missed the STEMI.\"</p></blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Try:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">\"Let's go back through that call. What findings did you notice? What made you think it wasn't cardiac? Here's what I was seeing that made me concerned about a STEMI.\"</p></blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Now you're teaching clinical reasoning instead of simply pointing out failure.</p><h3 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">One Rule: No Surprises</h3><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If a trainee is struggling, they shouldn't find out on their final evaluation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Have difficult conversations early.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Be honest.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Be respectful.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And give them a path forward.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The goal of feedback isn't to make the trainee feel bad. It's to make the trainee better.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Document the Process</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Field training documentation isn't just paperwork.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">It creates a record of:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Performance</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Feedback</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Areas needing improvement</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Progress</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Goals</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Follow-up conversations</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If a trainee is struggling, document the problem <strong>and the plan to improve it.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If they're doing exceptionally well, document that too.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Good documentation helps the trainee, the FTO, and the agency.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Check In Regularly</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't wait until the end of field training to ask:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"How do you think you're doing?\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Have regular conversations.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Ask:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What do you feel confident about?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What are you struggling with?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What can I do differently to help you learn?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Is the feedback I'm giving you useful?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>What do you want more practice with?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This creates a two-way learning environment instead of making field training feel like an interrogation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And don't forget that trainees need time away from EMS too.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Rest is part of learning.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The Bottom Line</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Being an FTO isn't just about evaluating whether someone can perform the job.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">It's about helping them <strong>become the provider they're capable of becoming.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You have an opportunity to shape someone's career.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">So when you're frustrated with your trainee, remember:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>You were new once too.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You had questions.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You made mistakes.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You probably had moments where you weren't sure you belonged.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Someone gave you an opportunity to learn.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Now it's your turn.</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Be the FTO you needed when you were new.</strong></p></blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Teach.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Coach.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Correct.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Encourage.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And when patient safety allows it—</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Step back and let them become a medic.</strong></p></div>","date":"2026-08-07 18:28:15","category":"EMS Education"},{"title":"etco2: The Vital Sign You Should Be Watching","slug":"etco2-the-vital-sign-you-should-be-watching","html":"<div class=\"sqs-html-content\" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Pulse oximetry tells you about oxygenation. Capnography tells you much more.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">In the prehospital environment, we don't have the luxury of a complete laboratory workup or unlimited diagnostic testing.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">That's why <strong>waveform capnography is such a powerful tool.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography provides a continuous, real-time measurement of exhaled carbon dioxide (CO₂). It can provide information about <strong>ventilation, perfusion, metabolism, airway placement, and response to treatment.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The key is learning to use the <strong>number, waveform, and trend</strong> together.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">ETCO₂ vs. SpO₂</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">SpO₂ primarily tells you about <strong>oxygenation</strong>.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">ETCO₂ gives you information about <strong>ventilation</strong> and can also provide clues about perfusion and metabolism.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This distinction matters.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A patient can have a normal SpO₂ while their ventilation is deteriorating—particularly when supplemental oxygen is being administered.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography can identify changes in ventilation much sooner than waiting for oxygen saturation to fall.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Think of it this way:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>SpO₂ asks: \"Are they oxygenating?\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ asks: \"Are they ventilating—and what might their circulation be telling us?\"</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Airway Confirmation</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">One of the most important uses of continuous waveform capnography is confirming and monitoring advanced airway placement.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">After placing an endotracheal tube or supraglottic airway, waveform capnography provides objective evidence that exhaled CO₂ is reaching the monitoring device.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But don't make the mistake of thinking:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"I saw CO₂ once, so I'm done.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The airway can become displaced.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The patient can deteriorate.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The tube can become obstructed.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The connection can come apart.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Continuous waveform capnography allows you to monitor the airway throughout patient care and transport.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A sudden loss or significant change in the waveform should make you stop and reassess.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography During Cardiac Arrest</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography becomes especially valuable during cardiac arrest.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Continuous ETCO₂ can help you evaluate:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Quality of CPR</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Advanced airway placement</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Changes in perfusion</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC)</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Deterioration after ROSC</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">During CPR, ETCO₂ provides a continuous indicator that can help you assess whether compressions are generating effective circulation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A <strong>sudden, sustained increase in ETCO₂</strong> can be an early clue that ROSC has occurred—prompting you to immediately reassess the patient for a pulse and other signs of circulation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Likewise, persistently low or falling values should make you reconsider the quality of CPR and the patient's overall perfusion.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The Waveform Can Tell You More Than the Number</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't become obsessed with the number on the monitor.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Look at the waveform.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The shape of the capnogram can provide important information about what is happening with the patient's ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">For example, patients experiencing significant bronchospasm from conditions such as asthma or COPD may develop a characteristic <strong>\"shark-fin\" waveform</strong> caused by obstructed exhalation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">As bronchospasm improves with treatment, the waveform may improve as well.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This gives you something incredibly valuable in the field:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Real-time feedback on whether your treatment is working.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography and Ventilation</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">ETCO₂ can also help guide ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If a patient is being ventilated manually or mechanically, capnography gives you immediate feedback about changes in ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Instead of simply watching the chest rise and fall, you're getting information about what is actually happening with exhaled CO₂.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This becomes particularly important after intubation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A patient can have a perfectly placed tube and still be ventilated poorly.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The tube being in the trachea doesn't mean you're ventilating the patient correctly.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography helps you continuously evaluate that process.</p><h1 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography and Sepsis</h1><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Here's where capnography becomes especially interesting for the prehospital provider.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ can provide clues about a patient's perfusion status—and that can be valuable when evaluating a patient with suspected sepsis.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">In sepsis, profound physiologic changes can occur, including <strong>vasodilation, impaired circulation, and altered cellular metabolism.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">As perfusion deteriorates, the amount of CO₂ delivered to the lungs can decrease, potentially resulting in a lower ETCO₂.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A <strong>low ETCO₂ in a patient with suspected infection, abnormal vital signs, and other signs of poor perfusion should increase your concern for significant illness.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But here's the important part:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ does not diagnose sepsis.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">You shouldn't look at an ETCO₂ of 25 mmHg and say, \"This patient is septic.\"</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Instead, use it as another piece of information.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Ask:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Does the patient have a suspected infection?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Are they tachycardic?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Are they hypotensive or trending toward hypotension?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is their mental status changing?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Are they showing signs of poor perfusion?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is their ETCO₂ unexpectedly low?</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Is the ETCO₂ changing as I treat the patient?</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Trend matters.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If your interventions improve perfusion and the patient's ETCO₂ rises along with other signs of clinical improvement, that may provide additional evidence that your resuscitation is having an effect.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography and Internal Hemorrhage</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Now consider the patient with <strong>occult or internal hemorrhage.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Maybe there's no obvious external bleeding.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The patient simply looks sick.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">They're pale.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">They're tachycardic.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Their blood pressure may still be \"normal.\"</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And their ETCO₂ is unexpectedly low.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Why might that happen?</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Remember where CO₂ comes from:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Cells produce CO₂ → blood transports it → the lungs exhale it.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If significant blood loss reduces circulating volume and cardiac output, less CO₂ may be transported to the lungs.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The result can be a <strong>decrease in ETCO₂.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This is why a falling ETCO₂ can be concerning in a patient who may be experiencing hemorrhagic shock.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And here's the important prehospital lesson:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Don't wait for hypotension to recognize shock.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A patient can compensate for significant blood loss before their blood pressure finally falls.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">If you have a patient with a mechanism or presentation concerning for internal bleeding and you see:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Tachycardia + altered mental status + cool/pale skin + poor peripheral perfusion + falling ETCO₂</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">you should be thinking seriously about <strong>circulatory compromise</strong>, even if the blood pressure hasn't collapsed yet.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Again, ETCO₂ cannot tell you:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"This patient is bleeding internally.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But it can provide another physiologic clue that something is wrong.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Watch the Trend</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This is one of the most important concepts with capnography.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Don't just look at the ETCO₂ value once.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Watch what happens over time.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">For example:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ 34 → 30 → 26</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">That's a different clinical story than:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ 26 → 30 → 34</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The first trend may indicate worsening perfusion or ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The second may indicate improvement.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The number doesn't exist in isolation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>The trend tells the story.</strong></p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't Ignore Perfusion</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">ETCO₂ isn't just about the lungs.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">CO₂ is produced by cellular metabolism and transported through the circulation to the lungs, where it is exhaled.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">That means ETCO₂ is influenced by more than ventilation.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">It is also affected by <strong>circulation and metabolism.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A significant decrease in ETCO₂ can therefore be a warning sign of worsening perfusion in the appropriate clinical context.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">This can be particularly useful when assessing patients with:</p><ul data-rte-list=\"default\"><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Shock</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Sepsis</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Hemorrhage</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Cardiac arrest</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Cardiogenic states</p></li><li><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Other causes of decreased cardiac output</p></li></ul><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But remember:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>ETCO₂ is a piece of the puzzle—not the entire puzzle.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Always interpret the number and waveform alongside the patient's clinical presentation, blood pressure, respiratory status, SpO₂, mental status, skin signs, pulse quality, and other available data.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A Better Way to Think About Capnography</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Don't think of capnography as:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>\"That thing I put on the tube.\"</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Think of it as a <strong>continuous window into the patient's physiology.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Ask yourself:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Is the patient ventilating?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Is the airway still in place?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Is my treatment improving their ventilation?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Are my compressions generating circulation?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Did ROSC occur?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Could this patient be developing shock?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Could a low or falling ETCO₂ support my concern for sepsis or hemorrhage?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Has the waveform changed?</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">These questions turn capnography from a monitor you attach to a patient into a clinical tool you actively use.</p><h2 style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">The Bottom Line</h2><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Capnography gives prehospital providers something incredibly valuable:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Real-time information about what's happening inside the patient.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">Use it to:</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Confirm the airway.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Monitor ventilation.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Evaluate CPR.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Recognize possible ROSC.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Trend perfusion.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Identify changes that may support concern for shock, sepsis, or hemorrhage.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Evaluate treatment response.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">And most importantly:</p><blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Don't just look at the ETCO₂ number. Look at the waveform, watch the trend, and connect what you see to what's happening with your patient.</strong></p></blockquote><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">A low ETCO₂ doesn't tell you exactly what's wrong.</p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\">But when you combine it with the patient's history, physical exam, vital signs, and clinical presentation, it may give you an important clue that <strong>your patient is becoming critically ill before the rest of the picture becomes obvious.</strong></p><p style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;\" data-rte-preserve-empty=\"true\"><strong>Capnography isn't just another number on the monitor. It's another set of eyes on your patient.</strong></p></div>","date":"2026-08-07 16:35:04","category":"EMS Education"},{"title":"Sepsis: Why Blood Vessels Dilate and When Fluids Aren't Enough","slug":"sepsis-vasodilation-fluids-vasopressors","date":"2026-10-27","category":"Sepsis & Shock","html":"<p>Septic shock is not simply a patient who needs more fluid. It is a complex form of circulatory failure involving widespread inflammation, loss of vascular tone, capillary leak, and sometimes myocardial dysfunction.</p><h2>How inflammation causes vasodilation</h2><p>During sepsis, the dysregulated response to infection releases inflammatory mediators including tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6). These signals can increase expression of inducible nitric oxide synthase (iNOS), resulting in excess nitric oxide (NO) production.</p><p>Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylate cyclase. This increases cyclic GMP (cGMP), promoting smooth muscle relaxation and vasodilation. Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) falls, and mean arterial pressure (MAP) can decline even if cardiac output is preserved or elevated. Other mediators and reduced vascular responsiveness to constrictors also contribute.</p><h2>Why septic patients need fluids</h2><p>Inflammation disrupts the endothelial barrier and glycocalyx, increasing capillary permeability. Fluid can leave the circulation and enter surrounding tissues, reducing effective circulating volume. IV crystalloids can improve venous return, preload, and cardiac output when a patient is fluid responsive.</p><p>However, fluid does not directly reverse nitric oxide–mediated vasodilation. Excess fluid may worsen edema and oxygenation. Reassess blood pressure, mental status, perfusion, respiratory findings, and signs of fluid responsiveness after resuscitation rather than giving repeated boluses automatically.</p><h2>When vasopressors matter</h2><p>Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor for septic shock. Through alpha-1 adrenergic stimulation, it increases vascular smooth muscle contraction, restores vascular tone, and raises SVR and MAP. It helps counter the consequences of vasodilation; it does not eliminate the underlying infection or nitric oxide production.</p><p>For adults requiring vasopressors, an initial MAP target around 65 mmHg is commonly recommended, individualized to the patient. In severe unstable shock, vasopressors may be started alongside fluids rather than waiting for a fixed fluid volume. Vasopressin may be added when norepinephrine alone is insufficient, using a separate V1 receptor pathway.</p><h2>What this means in the field</h2><p>Ask whether hypotension reflects reduced effective circulating volume, abnormal vascular tone, myocardial dysfunction, or a combination. Fluids address the volume component when responsive; vasopressors address inadequate vascular tone. Early recognition, frequent reassessment, timely infection treatment, and adherence to local EMS protocols are essential.</p><blockquote><p>The goal is not just a better blood pressure number. The goal is adequate tissue perfusion without unnecessary harm.</p></blockquote><p><em>Educational content for clinicians; follow current guidelines, local protocols, and medical direction.</em></p>"},{"title":"Right Ventricular Strain on the ECG: What S1Q3T3 Really Tells Us","slug":"right-ventricular-strain-ecg-s1q3t3-pulmonary-embolism","html":"<p><strong>A 12-lead ECG can tell us that the right ventricle is under pressure. It cannot tell us, by itself, why.</strong></p>\n<p>Picture a patient with sudden shortness of breath, tachycardia, and chest discomfort. The ECG shows deep T-wave inversions in the right precordial leads. Is it pulmonary embolism (PE)? Maybe. But the more useful first question is: <strong>What is happening to the right ventricle?</strong></p>\n<h2>Why the right ventricle becomes strained</h2>\n<p>The right ventricle (RV) is designed to pump blood into a relatively low-pressure pulmonary circulation. When pulmonary vascular resistance rises abruptly, the RV has to generate more pressure to move blood forward. Acute pulmonary embolism can create this obstruction, especially with a large clot burden. The RV may dilate, its wall tension and oxygen demand rise, and its electrical activation and repolarization can change.</p>\n<p>As RV output falls, less blood reaches the left ventricle. In severe cases, this contributes to reduced cardiac output, hypotension, and obstructive shock. This is why RV strain is more than an interesting ECG finding: it can be a clue to a patient with limited hemodynamic reserve.</p>\n<h2>ECG clues to right ventricular strain</h2>\n<p>Look for a <strong>pattern</strong>, not one magical lead:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>T-wave inversions in V1–V3 or V1–V4:</strong> Anterior/right-precordial repolarization abnormalities can reflect RV pressure overload. T-wave inversions may also extend into II, III, and aVF.</li>\n<li><strong>Right bundle branch block (RBBB):</strong> New incomplete or complete RBBB can occur when acute RV dilation affects conduction.</li>\n<li><strong>Right axis deviation:</strong> A rightward QRS axis may accompany RV pressure overload, although it has many other causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Sinus tachycardia:</strong> Common in PE but extremely nonspecific. The rate alone never establishes the diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong>QR pattern in V1 or S1Q3T3:</strong> Additional possible signs of acute right-heart strain, particularly in more significant presentations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> These findings can occur with conditions other than PE, including pulmonary hypertension and other causes of RV overload. A normal or nonspecific ECG does not rule out PE.</p>\n<h2>S1Q3T3: the famous pattern</h2>\n<p>Most of us learned to associate S1Q3T3 with pulmonary embolism. But what does it actually mean?</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>S1:</strong> A prominent S wave in lead I.</li>\n<li><strong>Q3:</strong> A Q wave in lead III.</li>\n<li><strong>T3:</strong> An inverted T wave in lead III.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Together, these findings are traditionally called the <strong>McGinn–White sign</strong>. They can reflect an acute shift in electrical forces associated with right-heart strain. <strong>S1Q3T3 is not a diagnosis of PE.</strong> It may be absent in patients with proven PE, and it can appear in other conditions that strain the right heart.</p>\n<p>A 2025 systematic review and meta-analysis of 24 studies (7,467 patients with acute PE) estimated that S1Q3T3 appeared in about <strong>15%</strong> of cases, with substantial variation across studies. That means the majority of patients with PE did <em>not</em> show this classic pattern.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Do not wait for S1Q3T3 to consider PE. And do not diagnose PE just because you see it.</strong></p></blockquote>\n<h2>What should make you more concerned?</h2>\n<p>Consider a patient with sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, syncope, unexplained tachycardia, hypoxemia, or signs of shock. Risk factors such as recent surgery, immobility, prior venous thromboembolism, active cancer, or unilateral leg swelling may increase concern. None of these findings is required for PE to be present.</p>\n<p>If the ECG also shows new right-precordial T-wave inversions, RBBB, or S1Q3T3, those findings may strengthen concern for RV strain. They should prompt you to reassess perfusion, blood pressure trends, mental status, oxygenation, and the overall clinical picture—not to declare a diagnosis from the ECG.</p>\n<h2>Why this matters in the field</h2>\n<p><strong>First, identify instability.</strong> Hypotension, altered mental status, syncope, or signs of poor perfusion in a patient with suspected PE raise concern for a high-risk presentation. Reassess frequently and prioritize timely transport and early notification according to local protocols.</p>\n<p><strong>Second, remember the RV is vulnerable.</strong> In severe RV failure, excessive positive-pressure ventilation or indiscriminate large-volume fluid administration can worsen hemodynamics. Support oxygenation and ventilation when needed, but tailor interventions to the patient's physiology and your protocols. Do not delay necessary airway support.</p>\n<p><strong>Third, use the ECG as one piece of evidence.</strong> A 12-lead can help identify alternative emergencies such as acute coronary syndrome, reveal dysrhythmias, and raise suspicion for RV strain. Definitive PE evaluation requires clinical probability assessment and appropriate testing, often including imaging.</p>\n<h2>The bottom line</h2>\n<p>When you see right ventricular strain on an ECG, don't just memorize the pattern. Understand the physiology behind it: <strong>the right ventricle may be struggling against increased resistance.</strong></p>\n<p>S1Q3T3 is worth recognizing, but right-precordial T-wave inversions, new conduction changes, and—most importantly—the patient's presentation deserve your attention. <strong>Treat the patient, not the mnemonic.</strong></p>\n<h2>References and further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/41/4/543/5556136\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2019 ESC Guidelines for acute pulmonary embolism</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12251390/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2025 systematic review and meta-analysis of ECG abnormalities in acute PE</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40586314/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2025 study of diagnostic accuracy of classical PE ECG findings</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11154926/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Intermediate- and high-risk PE: recognition and management</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Educational content for EMS clinicians; follow local medical direction and protocols.</em></p>","date":"2026-10-27","category":"Cardiology & EKG"}];